1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muwatalli_II
2 CONT
2 CONT Muwatalli II was a king of the New Kingdom of the Hittite empire (ca. 1295–1272 BC .
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son of Mursili II and Queen Gassulawiya, and he had several siblings.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is best known as the Hittite ruler who fought Ramesses II to a standstill at the Battle of Kadesh around 1274 BC. Aside from the battle with Egypt, he is best known for relocating the Hittite capital to Tarhuntassa and appointi
2 CONC ng his brother Hattusili as governor in Hattusa.
2 CONT
2 CONT A copy of a treaty has been recovered between him and Alaksandu, ruler of Wilusa (Troy), one of the Arzawa lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Egyptologists suspect that some time prior to Ramesses II's accession to the Egyptian throne, Muwattalli had reached an informal peace treaty or understanding with Seti I over Kadesh to avoid a clash between the two powers over co
2 CONC ntrol of Syria. In it, Seti effectively ceded Kadesh to the Hittite king in order to focus on domestic issues in Egypt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muwatalli had a wife named Tanu-Ḫepa and at least two children. One was Urhi-Teshup, who became king as Mursili III until his uncle Hattusili III deposed him. Another was Kurunta who became the vassal ruler of Tarhuntassa during t
2 CONC he reign of Hattusili III. Another person named Ulmi-Teshup is suggested to be a third son of Muwatalli II but it is quite likely that Ulmi-Teshup and Kurunta are the same person.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tudhaliya IV and Egyptian Queen Maathorneferure were the nephew and niece of Muwatalli.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muwatalli's namesake, Muwatalli I, was a pre-Empire king of the early 14th century, the predecessor of Tudhaliya I.
