1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Puduhepa From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT The figure on the right is queen Puduhepa
2 CONT
2 CONT Other names
2 CONT
2 CONT Pudu-Kheba
2 CONT
2 CONT Title
2 CONT  Queen of the Hittites
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse(s)
2 CONT  King Hattusili III
2 CONT
2 CONT Children Tudhaliya IV
2 CONT  Maathorneferure
2 CONT  Kiluš-Ḫepa
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents
2 CONT  Bentepsharri
2 CONT
2 CONT Puduhepa (Hittite: Pudu-Ḫepa, or Pudu-Kheba) was a Hittite Queen married to the King Hattusili III. She has been referred to as "one of the most influential women known from the Ancient Near East."[1][2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Sources
2 CONT  3 See also
2 CONT  4 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Early life and marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Puduhepa was born at the beginning of the 13th century BC in the city of Lawazantiya in Kizzuwatna (i.e. Cilicia, a region south of the Hittite kingdom). Her father Bentepsharri was the head priest of the tutelary divinity of the 
2 CONC city, Ishtar, and Puduhepa grew up to exercise the function of priestess of this same goddess.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his return from the Battle of Kadesh, the Hittite general Hattusili met Puduhepa and, it was said, Ishtar instructed him to take her as his wife. She went with him then to the kingdom of Hapissa. When her spouse successfully ro
2 CONC se to the Hittite throne by defeating his nephew Mursili III, Puduhepa became a queen.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT Puduhepa had an important role to play in the Hittite court and international diplomacy of the period. She would appear constantly by the side of her husband as he made his rulings and decisions. It appears however that she was po
2 CONC rtrayed reigning hand in hand with her husband rather than subservient to the king.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT She played an important role in diplomacy with Ancient Egypt. Puduhepa communicated several times with the king of Egypt Ramesses II as he signed a peace treaty with Hattusili, in which Hattusili agrees that two of his daughters s
2 CONC hould go to Egypt to marry Ramesses.
2 CONT
2 CONT A diplomatic marriage between King Kadashman-Enlil II of Babylonia may have been in the offing with Puduhepa matchmaking Kadashman-Enlil’s betrothal to one of her daughters.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT A princess of Babylon was married into the Hittite royal family. This would have been a daughter or sister of King Kudur-Enlil and the news elicited contempt from Ramesses II, who apparently no longer regarded Babylon significant.
2 CONC  Puduhepa replied in a letter,[6] ‘If you say "The king of Babylon is not a Great King," then you do not know the status of Babylon.’[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Hattusili, the role of Puduhepa expanded under the reign of her son Tudhaliya IV, under the title of goddess-queen. She was involved in judicial matters to the point of intervening in legal cases. She was also a
2 CONC  priestess who worked on organising and rationalising Hittite religion.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her daughters were Queen Maathorneferure[9] of Egypt and Princess Kiluš-Ḫepa.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nefertari sent gifts to Puduhepa.  [edit] Nefertari and Puduhepa
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Nefertari of Egypt sent gifts to Puduhepa:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The great Queen Naptera (Nefertari) of the land of Egypt speaks thus: ʻSpeak to my sister Puduhepa, the Great Queen of the Hatti land. I, your sister, (also) be well!! May your country be well. Now, I have learned that you, my si
2 CONC ster, have written to me asking after my health. You have written to me because of the good friendship and brotherly relationship between your brother, the king of Egypt, The Great and the Storm God will bring about peace, and he 
2 CONC will make the brotherly relationship between the Egptian king, the Great King, and his brother, the Hatti King, the Great King, last for ever... See, I have sent you a gift, in order to greet you, my sister... for your neck (a nec
2 CONC klace) of pure gold, composed of 12 bands and weighing 88 shekels, coloured linen maklalu-material, for one royal dress for the king... A total of 12 linen garments.’"[11][12][13]
2 CONT
2 CONT Maathorneferure in Egypt bore a daughter named Neferure.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Sources
2 CONT
