1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilukhepa
2 CONT
2 CONT For political reasons, Gilukhipa was sent to Egypt to join Amenhotep III in marriage. The Egyptian pharaoh made a special issue of commemorative scarabs on the occasion of his marriage to Gilukhipa in his 10th regnal year, where h
2 CONC e recorded that the princess was escorted by 317 ladies-in-waiting, women from the Mitanni king's royal palace.[2] Gilukhipa became known as the "Secondary King's Wife," meaning she was secondary to Amenhotep III's chief wife, Que
2 CONC en Tiye.
2 CONT
2 CONT Twenty-six years later, her niece Tadukhepa also became Amenhotep's wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT References	Edit
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Wolfgang Helck, Urkunden der 18. Dynastie 1957, p. 1738 ^ Aidan Dodson & Dyan Hilton, The Complete Royal Families of Ancient Egypt, Thames & Hudson (2004), p.154
