1 NOTE Ikhnaton was the last important ruler of the 18th Dynasty and notable as 
2 CONT the first historical figure to establish a religion based on the concept 
2 CONT of monotheism. He established the cult of Aton, or Aten, the sun god or 
2 CONT solar disk, which he believed to be a universal, omnipresent spirit and 
2 CONT the sole creator of the universe.  After he established the new religion, 
2 CONT sometimes referred to as solar monotheism, he changed his name from the 
2 CONT royal designation Amenhotep IV to Ikhnaton, meaning "Aton is satisfied." 
2 CONT He moved his capital from Thebes to Akhetaton, or Tell el-Amarna, a new 
2 CONT city devoted to the celebration of Aton, and he ordered the obliteration 
2 CONT of all traces of the polytheistic religion of his ancestors. He also 
2 CONT fought bitterly against the powerful priests who attempted to maintain the 
2 CONT worship of the state god Amon, or Amen. This religious revolution had a 
2 CONT profound effect on Egyptian artists, who turned from the ritualistic forms 
2 CONT to which they had been confined to a much more realistic representation of 
2 CONT nature as evidence of the all-embracing power of the sun, Aton. A new 
2 CONT religious literature also arose. This blossoming of culture, however, did 
2 CONT not survive Ikhnaton. Under his son-in-law, Tutankhamen, the capital was 
2 CONT moved back to Thebes; the old polytheistic religion was restored; and 
2 CONT Egyptian art once more became ritualized.
