2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Khepermare Ramesses X (also written Ramses and Rameses) (ruled c. 1111 BC – 1107 BC)[1] was the ninth ruler of the 20th dynasty of Ancient Egypt. His birth name was Amonhirkhepeshef. It is uncertain if his reign was 3 or 4 Years, 
2 CONC but there is now a strong consensus among Egyptologists that it did not last as long as 9 Years, as was previously assumed. His prenomen or throne name, Khepermaatre, means "The Justice of Re Abides."[2] He was possibly a son of R
2 CONC amesses IX and husband of Tyti, but this is unproven. The English Egyptologist Aidan Dodson states:
2 CONT
2 CONT "No evidence is known to indicate the relationship between the final kings Ramesses IX, X and XI. If they were a father-son succession, Tyti, who bears the titles of King's Daughter, King's Wife and King's Mother, would seem [to b
2 CONC e] a good candidate for the wife of Ramesses X, but little else can be discerned."[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramesses X is a poorly documented king. All that is really known about his kingship is that the general insecurity and wave of tomb robberies which had become prevalent under his predecessors continued to grow under his reign. His
2 CONC  Year 1 and Year 2 is attested by Papyrus Turin 1932+1939 while his third Year is documented in a diary kept by a Workmen of Deir El Medina.[4] The diary mentions the general idleness of the necropolis workmen due to the threat po
2 CONC sed by Libyan marauders in the Valley of the Kings. It records that the Deir El-Medina workmen were absent from work in Year 3 IIIrd Month of Peret (ie: Winter) days 6, 9, 11, 12, 18, 21 and 24 for fear of the "desert-dwellers" (i
2 CONC e: the Libyans or Meshwesh) who evidently roamed through Upper Egypt and Thebes at will.[5] This is partly a reflection of the massive Libyan influx into the Western Delta region of Lower Egypt during this time. Ramesses X is also
2 CONC  the last New Kingdom king whose rule over Nubia is attested from an inscription at Aniba.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT His KV18 tomb in the Valley of the Kings was left unfinished and it is uncertain if he was ever buried here since no remains or fragments of funerary objects were discovered within it.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Amonhirkhepeshef Khepermare, The Justice of Re Abides, Rameses X, Pharaoh of Egypt, died circa 1098 BC.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.american-pictures.com/genealogy/persons/per00699.htm#0
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT His third Year is documented in a diary kept by a Workmen of Deir El Medina. The diary mentions the general idleness of the necropolis workmen due to the threat posed by Libyan marauders in the Valley of the Kings. Ramesses X is a
2 CONC lso the last New Kingdom king whose rule over Nubia is attested--from an inscription at Aniba. His KV18 tomb in the Valley of the Kings was left unfinished and it is uncertain if he was ever buried here since no remains or fragmen
2 CONC ts of funerary objects were discovered within it.
2 CONT --------------------
