2 CONT Shalmaneser IFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopediaJump to: navigation, search   King Shalmaneser I, pouring out Dust of a Conquered City in front of an Assyrian Temple after returning victorious.Shalmaneser I (Shulmanu-asharedu;[1
2 CONC ] 1274 BC – 1245 BC or 1265 BC - 1235 BC) was a king of Assyria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Adad-nirari I, he succeeded his father as king in 1265 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to his annals, discovered at Assur, in his first year he conquered eight countries in the northwest and destroyed the fortress of Arinnu, the dust of which he brought to Assur. In his second year he defeated Shattuara, k
2 CONC ing of Hanilgalbat, and his Hittite and Ahlamu allies. He incorporated the remains of the Mittani kingdom as part of one of the Assyrian provinces. Shalmaneser I also claimed to have blinded 14,400 enemy prisoners in one eye. He w
2 CONC as one of the first Assyrian kings who was known to deport his defeated enemies to various lands rather than simply slaughtering them all.
2 CONT
2 CONT He conquered the whole country from Taidu to Irridu, from Mount Kashiar to Eluhat, and from the fortresses of Sudu and Harranu to Carchemish on the Euphrates. He built palaces at Assur and Nineveh, restored the "world-temple" at A
2 CONC ssur, and founded the city of Nimrud. He was succeeded by his son Tukulti-Ninurta I.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Limmu Officials by YearAnnual limmu officials beginning with the year of accession of Šulmanu-ašared. The list is partly derived from Freydank[2] and McIntyre.[3] The exact order of the earliest limmus is conjectural but th
2 CONC e ordering from Šerriya onwards is essentially fixed.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1265 Adad-šumu-lešir son of Sin-ašared 1264 Šulmanu-ašared (king) 1263 Mušabšiu-Šibitti 1262 Ber-šumu-iddina 1261 Abi-ili son Aššur-šumu-lešir 1260 Aššur-alik-pana 1259 Adad-Šamši son of Adad-šumu-lešir 1258 Kidin-Sin son Adad-tey
