1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ninurta-tukulti-AshurFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopediaJump to: navigation, search Ninurta-tukultī-Aššur, inscribed mdNinurta2-tukul-ti-Aš-šur, was briefly king of Assyria during 1133 BC, the 84th to appear on t
2 CONC he Assyrian Kinglist, marked as holding the throne for his ṭuppišu, “his tablet,” a period thought to correspond just to the inauguration year. He succeeded his father, the long-reigning Aššur-dān I, but the throne was very quickl
2 CONC y usurped by his brother, Mutakkil-Nusku and he was driven from Assur and sought refuge in the city of Sišil, on the Babylonian border, the scene of the final dénouement.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] BiographyThere is some conjecture that he may have ruled jointly with Aššur-dan I during Aššur-dan's declining years or perhaps shared some regnal duties as there is a significant archive of administrative texts[i 1] concer
2 CONC ning agricultural products, (from cities such as Arrapha), food distribution, and ritual offerings, in the royal palace referencing him and his wife Rimeni on seals, one of which provides an early Assyrian chariot scene, but only 
2 CONC three of these texts call him king.[1] Among these there is a reference to the partial demolition of a number of buildings in Kar-Tukulti-Ninurta, during his reign,[2] and also a harem list. The Chronicle P[i 2] which names him mT
2 CONC ukul-ti-Aššur, relates that during his reign the statue of Marduk was returned to Babylon having languished in Assyria for sixty (?)-six years, something of an underestimate if the reading of this number is correct and a reconcili
2 CONC atory move likely to test his subjects loyalty.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Assyrian King List[i 3] relates that “Mutakkil-Nusku, his brother, fought with him (and) carried him off to Karduniaš. Mutakkil-Nusku held the throne for “his tablet” (and then) passed away.”[i 4][4] A fairly recently recovere
2 CONC d source[i 5] provides additional insight into these events. From it we learn Ninurta-tukultī-Aššur seems to have kept the loyalty of provincial regions, while the heartland of Assyria sided with Mutakkil-Nusku, fueling a civil wa
2 CONC r.[5] Into this milieu comes fragments[i 6] of one, or perhaps two letters, from a Babylonian king, tentatively identified as Ninurta-nādin-šumi, although his predecessor Itti-Marduk-balāṭu or his successor Nabû-kudurrī-uṣur I cou
2 CONC ld also conceivably be the author, addressed to and lambasting Mutakkil-Nusku and threatening to reinstate Ninurta-tukultī-Aššur. His Babylonian contemporary is not certain and the Synchronistic Kinglist[i 7] gives a counterpart w
2 CONC hose name begins with, or contains, the theophoric element Marduk-, with other fragmentary copies[i 8][i 9] providing no further insight. The text of the letter(s) is poorly preserved, and difficult to interpret, but the Babylonia
2 CONC n quotes the Assyrian in his description of his brother as ku-lu-‘-ú la zi-ka-ru šu-ú, “a kulu’u, not a man,” where the term may mean a 'feminized castrato cultic performer'.[6] The deadlock was apparently broken by the attack by 
2 CONC Mutakkil-Nusku’s forces on Ninurta-tukultī-Aššur’s border stronghold of Sišil, after which both brothers disappeared from history, perhaps falling in their fratricidal battle – and Mutakkil-Nusku’s son Aššur-reš-iši I assumed the 
2 CONC throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ninurta-tukulti-Ashur
