1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Wikipedia: [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pharaoh%27s_daughter_%28wife_of_Solomon%29#Naming_the_Pharaoh Father of Pharaoh's daughter.]
2 CONT * Source: [http://www.northforest.org/BiblicalArchaeology/solomon.html Biblical Archaeology - Solomon.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Pharaoh Haremheb was reigning during the early years of King Solomon. (pg 181)
2 CONT
2 CONT Previous to the time of Haremheb the Pharaohs would not have suffered the indignity of allowing an Egyptian princess to be married off to a foreign ruler. For example, Amenhotep II had once been asked by the king of Mitanni to sup
2 CONC ply one of his daughters to seal an alliance but he haughtily refused. (pg 184-185)
2 CONT
2 CONT However, if ever there were a time in Egyptian history when a Pharaoh's daughter might be offered to a foreign ruler it was during Haremheb's reign. Four reasons: (pg 185)
2 CONT
2 CONT # Haremheb was not directly descended from the royal bloodline and was not so overly burdened with the sense of dynastic purity. (pg 184).
2 CONT # Haremheb made his career in the military and was Commander-in-Chief of the Egyptian army. He was a practical man. (pg 184-185).
2 CONT # Egypt was no longer militarily strong enough to recover the Levant by a force of arms. It would be expedient to tie powerful Levantine city-state rulers to the Egyptian throne through marriage alliance perhaps for a mutually-ben
2 CONC eficial trading partnership. (pg 185).
2 CONT # The lady 'Sharelli' of the Pharaoh's court (almost certainly a princess) was married to one of the more powerful city-state rulers of Western Asia at around the same time. (pg 185).
