2 CONT http://webcache.googleusercontent.com/search?q=cache:lZ1nXCF0o1AJ:www.livius.org/na-nd/nabopolassar/nabopolassar.html+%22nabopolassar%22&cd=4&hl=no&ct=clnk&gl=no
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Nabopolassar (Akkadian:Nabû-apal-usur) (c.658 - 605 BC) was the first king of the Neo-Babylonian Empire.[1] He ruled over Babylon for 20 years (625 - 605 BC).
2 CONT
2 CONT Rise to Power
2 CONT
2 CONT He rose in revolt against the Assyrian Empire (which had ruled Babylon for the previous 200 years) in 627 BC, after the last significant Assyrian king, Ashurbanipal(ASH-ur-BA-Nee-paal), died in 626 BC. In 625 BC, the Assyrian Empi
2 CONC re's grasp on Babylon was now almost nonexistent, so he became its first king that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Destruction of Assyria
2 CONT
2 CONT The weakened Assyrians could not resist his power and that of the Medes, who combined to sack the Assyrian capital of Nineveh in 612 BC, at the Battle of Nineveh. Nabopolassar was left in control of Nineveh and destroyed the remna
2 CONC nts of the Assyrian Empire in 605 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other Campaigns
2 CONT
2 CONT Nabopolassar waged war against Egypt from 610 BC until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 610 BC, Nabopolassar took the Assyrian city of Harran, where Assyrian forces had retreated after the fall of Nineveh. Later that year, his son Nebuchadrezzar succeeded him to the throne of Babylonia and won the Battle of Carche
2 CONC mish, fought against Pharaoh Necho of Egypt, shortly before Nabopolassar died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later years
2 CONT
2 CONT For the last five years of his very productive life, Nebopolassar was leading the Babylonian army in a successful war against Egypt. Once victory was claimed, Nebopolassar, now in his fifties, gave up the throne in favor of his so
2 CONC n, Nebuchadnezzer II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Within months of his abdication in 605 BC, Nebopolassar died of natural causes. He was about 53 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nabopolassar
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT NabopolassarFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopediaJump to: navigation, search Nabopolassar (pron.: /ˌnæbɵpəˈlæsər/; Akkadian: Nebû-apal-usur; c. 658 BC – 605 BC) was the king of the Babylonia and played a key role in the demise of 
2 CONC the Assyrian Empire following the death of the last powerful Assyrian king, Ashurbanipal.[1] He ruled over Babylon for twenty years (625–605 BC).
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]  1 Military campaigns 2 Later years 3 References 4 See also 5 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Military campaignsNabopolassar revolted against the Assyrian Empire (which had ruled Babylon for the previous 200 years) after the death of the last effective Assyrian king, Ashurbanipal, some time between 631 BC and 627 BC
2 CONC . He then took on the title of King of Babylonia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Assyria, weakened by internal strife and ineffectual rule following the death of Ashurbanipal, was unable to resist the alliance of the Chaldeans and Medes, who combined to sack the Assyrian capital of Nineveh in 612 BC. Following
2 CONC  a prolonged siege at the Battle of Nineveh, Nabopolassar took control of Nineveh. In 609 BC, Nabopolassar took the Assyrian city of Harran, where Assyrian forces had retreated after the fall of Nineveh.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 610 BC until his death, Nabopolassar also waged war against Egypt, which was allied with Assyria. In 605 BC, his son Nebuchadnezzar fought Pharaoh Necho II of Egypt and the remnants of the Assyrian army at the Battle of Carch
2 CONC emish, shortly before Nabopolassar died.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Later yearsOnce his forces had defeated the Assyrians and their Egyptian allies, Nabopolassar gave up the throne in favour of his son, Nebuchadnezzar II. Within months of his abdication in 605 BC, Nabopolassar died of natur
2 CONC al causes at about 53 years of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] References1.^ D. Brendan Nagle, The Ancient World: A Social and Cultural History, 6th ed., Upper Saddle River, N.J.: Pearson, 58. [edit] See alsoBabylonia Kings of Babylonia [edit] External linksABC 2: Chronicle Concerning 
