1 NOTE d. 9/3/401 BATTLE OF KOUNAXA
1 NOTE Cyrus the Younger (424-401 bc), Persian prince, son of Darius II, king of 
2 CONT Persia, and brother of Artaxerxes II (reigned 404-358? bc). In 408 bc he 
2 CONT was made satrap (governor) of the Persian provinces in western Asia Minor 
2 CONT and was ordered to assist the forces of Sparta during the last years of 
2 CONT the Peloponnesian War. When Darius died and Artaxerxes succeeded (404 bc) 
2 CONT to the throne, Cyrus planned a revolt, but his plan was revealed by 
2 CONT Tissaphernes (f. 413-395 bc), the satrap of Caria. Cyrus was pardoned 
2 CONT through the influence of his mother, Parysatis (died 395? bc), and sent 
2 CONT back to his satrapy. There he collected a force of about 100,000 Persian 
2 CONT subjects and 13,000 Greeks, most of the latter Spartans influenced by his 
2 CONT aid in the Peloponnesian War. Under the pretext of leading an expedition 
2 CONT against bandits in Pisidia, he set forth from Sardis toward Babylon, the 
2 CONT Persian capital. In 401 bc the armies of Artaxerxes met those of Cyrus in 
2 CONT battle at Cunaxa, near the Euphrates River, and Cyrus was killed while 
2 CONT fighting. An important repercussion of this battle was the strategic 
2 CONT retreat of the Greeks through the heart of Persian territory to the Black 
2 CONT Sea, exposing the military weakness of the Persians to the Greek world. 
2 CONT The story of Cyrus's revolt and of the march of the Ten Thousand Greeks 
2 CONT was told by the Athenian general and historian Xenophon in the Anabasis.
