1 NOTE OR "BARSINE"; d. 326 BC
1 NOTE Barsine (363?-309): Persian princess, mistress of the Macedonian kingAlexander the Great.
2 CONT Barsine was probably born in 363 as the oldest daughter of an importantPersian nobleman, Artabazus. She had a full brother Pharnabazus, who wasperhaps a little older. Like his ancestors, Artabazus served as satrap ofHellespontine 
2 CONC Phrygia, a province of the Achaemenid empire in thenorthwest of modern Turkey. Barsine was educated by the best Persianteachers, but she also had Greeks teachers (see quote below).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 358, her father stood up against the new Persian king Artaxerxes IIIOchus. Because the latter still had to establish his rule in Persiaproper, Artabazus was first left alone; later, he was forced to hireGreek mercenaries. Among
2 CONC  these were two Rhodian brothers, Mentor andMemnon. The relation between them and Artabazus was close: in 355, therebel satrap married their sister and Mentor married to Barsine. He wasthe first of her three known lovers, but thei
2 CONC r marriage was not consumedyet, because Barsine was too young.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the first half of 354, a Persian army suppressed Artabazus' rebellion.With his daughter, his son, his wives and Memnon, the defeated rebel fledto Pella, the capital of Macedonia, where king Philip (356-336) welcomedthem and inv
2 CONC ited them to stay as long as they liked. The exiles met theyoung crown prince Alexander -seven or eight years younger than Barsine-and may have met the famous philosopher and biologist Aristotle, whoarrived in Pella a few months b
2 CONC efore they were recalled, in 343.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Barsine and her husbands
2 CONT They owed their recall to Barsine's husband. After the suppression ofArtabazus' revolt, Mentor had fled to Egypt (which was independent atthat time) but later, he had been able to regain Artaxerxes' favor. In343, he played an impo
2 CONC rtant role in the Persian reconquest of Egypt,which had been independent for some sixty years. When Artaxerxes askedhim how he could express his gratitude, the mercenary asked for therecall of Memnon, Artabazus and Barsine. The ki
2 CONC ng pardoned the formerrebels and received invaluable information about the plans of Philip toattack Persia as soon as he had subdued the Greek cities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barsine and her husband met each other again in 342; she was nowtwenty-one. They did not long enjoy each other's company, because Mentordied two years later. As far as we know, the couple had only one child, adaughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Almost immediately after the death of Mentor, she remarried, this time toMemnon, who had already several sons from an earlier marriage. In theseyears, Memnon lived in the Troad, where he owed land; he expected to beappointed supre
2 CONC me Persian commander in the West, but neither ArtaxerxesIII, nor Artaxerxes IV (338-336) nor Darius III (336-330) dared to givethe former rebel this prestigious job.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 334, the Macedonians invaded the Achaemenid empire, commanded byAlexander, who was now king. Memnon and his sons took part in the defenseof Asia, and although the Persians suffered a defeat at the Granicusriver, Memnon's qualit
2 CONC ies were too remarkable, and he was made supremecommander in the West. To keep him loyal, his wife had to stay at thecourt of king Darius.
2 CONT
2 CONT Memnon and (after his death in 333) Barsine's brother Pharnabazus,achieved remarkable successes in the war against Alexander: with 300ships, they controlled the Aegean sea, threatened to cut off Alexander'slines of supplies, made 
2 CONC the Spartans revolt and reached the point wherethey could bring the war to Macedonia. However, Alexander had moved tothe east and had defeated Darius near Issus (November 333). He continuedto besiege Tyre, where Pharnabazus' ships
2 CONC  came from, and in 332, thePersian fleet disintegrated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barsine and Darius' wives had been with Darius when he was defeated;Alexander's men caught the ladies. The Greek philosopher Plutarch ofChaeronea tells what happened next:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander, esteeming it more kingly to govern himself than to conquer hisenemies, sought no intimacy with any one of them, nor indeed with anyother women before marriage, except Barsine, Memnon's widow, who wastaken prisoner at Da
2 CONC mascus. She had been instructed in the Greeklearning, was of a gentle temper, and by her father, Artabazus, royallydescended, with good qualities, added to the solicitations andencouragement of [Alexander's general] Parmenion, as 
2 CONC Aristobulus tellsus, made him the more willing to attach himself to so agreeable andillustrious a woman.
2 CONT [Plutarch, Life of Alexander 21; tr. John Dryden]
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander was Barsine's third man, and their affair may have been agrown-up continuation of a childhood friendship. However, there may havebeen political considerations, too: after 330, Alexander wanted to berecognized as king of 
2 CONC Persia, and a Persian wife would certainly improvethe acceptance of his rule by the native population. At the same time,Barsine was not too strange: she spoke Greek, which made her a lot moreattractive than other Persian women, wh
2 CONC o were, in Alexander's opinion,terrible eyesores.
2 CONT
2 CONT A few weeks after the death of Darius in the desert east of Rhagae(modern Teheran), Alexander and his mistress were in Hyrcania, where theyreceived Artabazus: Barsine's father had always remained loyal to hisking but now decided t
2 CONC o switch sides.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 327, after Alexander had finished the pacification of Sogdiana,Barsine gave birth to Alexander's first-born child, a son that he calledHeracles. Almost immediately, he married to another woman: Roxane. Thiswas tactless, and und
2 CONC erstandably, Barsine did not remain at Alexander'scourt. She returned to the west, where she and her son lived in Pergamum.Mentor's daughter was married to Alexander's admiral, Nearchus, in 324.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 309, Polyperchon, one of the Diadochi who after Alexander's deathfought for a share of his empire, tried to restore Heracles to thethrone, but he was persuaded by Cassander to give up the attempt. Barsineand Heracles were execu
2 CONC ted, although it is possible that they had beenkilled earlier; in that case, the pretender of 309 was a 'false Heracles'.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT  Literature
2 CONT P.A. Brunt, 'Alexander, Barsine and Heracles,' in: Rivista di filologia edi istruzione classica 103 (1975) 22-34.
