1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Vonones I=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Vonones I of Parthia (Persian: ونون یکم‎‎), (ΟΝΩΝΗΣ on his coins) ruled the Parthian Empire from about 8 to 12 AD. He was the eldest son of Phraates IV of Parthia (ruled c. 37-2 BC) and was sent to Rome as a hostage in the 20s BC 
2 CONC as surety for a treaty his father made with Augustus.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ascension'''
2 CONT
2 CONT After the assassination of Orodes III in about 6 AD, the Parthians applied to Augustus for a new King from the house of Arsaces. Augustus sent them Vonones I, but he could not maintain himself as King; he had been educated as a Ro
2 CONC man, and was despised by the Parthian nobility as a Roman stooge.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Fall'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Another member of the Arsacid house, Artabanus III (ruled c. 10–38), who was living among the Dahan nomads in the east of Parthia, was invited to the throne. In a civil war he defeated and expelled Vonones I.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Coins'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The coins of Vonones I date from 8 to 12 AD and bear the inscription "King Vonones, conqueror of Artabanus" commemorating a temporary victory over his rival. Those of Artabanus II begin in the year 10. In about the year 12 Vonones
2 CONC  I fled into Armenia and became King there. Artabanus II demanded his deposition, and as Augustus did not wish to begin a war with the Parthians he moved Vonones I into Syria, where he was kept in custody, though in a kingly style
2 CONC . Later he was moved to Cilicia, and when he tried to escape in about 19 AD, he was killed by his guards.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vonones_I
