2 CONT Lang, David Marshall – Armenier: Ett folk i exil, Tryckeriförlaget, Täby, 1989
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The family of Chrosov ou Chosroes III (tg) d'ARMÉNIE and ..
2 CONT
2 CONT [128927] ARMÉNIE (d'), Chrosov ou Chosroes III (tg) (Trdat ou Tiridates (tg) III le Saint & Ashken d'ALANIE [128926]), roi d'Arménie, born about 281, died 339
2 CONT
2 CONT * married about 300
2 CONT
2 CONT .., .. (..)
2 CONT
2 CONT       1) Bambishn, princesse d'Arménie, born about 301, married about 320 Atanakines Souren Pahlav SUREN-PAHLAV
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliographie : Traité de généalogie (René Jetté) 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.francogene.com/quebec--genealogy/128/128927.php
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Khosrov III the Small (Armenian: Խոսրով Գ Կոտակ, Khosrov III Kotak; r. 330–339 A.D.) was the Arshakuni king Armenia. He was the son and successor of King Tiridates III and a member of the Arshakuni Dynasty. He was a man of short s
2 CONC tature, thus his name. He founded a hunting ground (which was named after him) and the city of Dvin, which later became the Armenian capital.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT During his reign, two generals, Vache Mamikonian and Vahan Amatuni, distinguished themselves for their valor in battle, often coming to help the king. During these years, pro-Sassanid and anti-Mamikonian sentiment grew in Armenia 
2 CONC and so did anti-Roman sentiment. Pro-Sassanid groups gained popularity so much so that they were successful in assassinating Catholicos St. Aristaces I, son of Gregory the Illuminator.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shapur II, Sassanid king of the Persians, invaded Armenia twice and did gain some territory. Vache Mamikonian was killed in those battles and was later named a saint by the Armenian Apostolic Church for his sacrifice. Khosrov died
2 CONC  in 339.
