1 NAME Amnius Anicius Julianus
2 GIVN Amnius Anicius Julianus
2 NICK Amnius Anicius Iulianus
2 NICK Roman Consul
1 SEX M
1 OCCU unknown
2 PLAC Consul 334, prefect of Rome 326-9
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 270
2 PLAC Rome, Italy
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 329
2 PLAC Rome, Italy
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Consul
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Amnius Anicius Julianus (Latin: Amnius Anicius Iulianus; floruit 322–329) was a politician of the Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography: Anicius Julianus was consul in 322 with Petronius Probianus, praefectus urbi Romae between 326 and 329.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is identified with the proconsul of Africa proconsularis who, in 302, received a rescript (an answer to a request for clarification that Julianus had sent) from emperor Diocletian, which ordered the suppression of the Manichees
2 CONC  in Africa, accused of being in contact with the Sasanid empire.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been proposed[2] that the Julianus proconsul of Africa could have been active in 296–297 and that he was put to death by Maximian with fabricated treason charges; his figure of rebel in Africa should be, therefore, linked t
2 CONC o Sabinus Iulianus, an usurper in Africa recorded in Aurelius Victor's Caesares 39.22.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Some recent prosopographical studies[3] have tried to reconstruct his family ties. According to these studies, Julianus, probably born around 270, was son of Sextus Anicius Faustus Paulinus, consul in 298, and younger brother of S
2 CONC extus Anicius Faustus Paulinus, consul in 325. He married Caesonia Manilia (born ca 275), daughter of Lucius Caesonius Ovinius Rufinus Manilius Bassus (or Rufinius), consul in 280; they had Amnius Manius Caesonius Nicomachus Anici
2 CONC us Paulinus Honorius, consul in 334.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ Collectio 15.3; Simon Corcoran, The empire of the tetrarchs: imperial pronouncements and government, AD 284–324, Oxford University Press, 2000, ISBN 0-19-815304-X, p. 135. ^ Bruce, L.D., "Diocletian, the Proconsul Iulianus and t
2 CONC he Manichaeans", in C. Deroux, Studies in Latin Literature and Roman History, iii (Collection Latomus, 180; Brusselles), 336–347. ^ Settipani.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Iulianus 23" Prosopography of the Later Roman Empire, Volume 1, pp. 473–4. Christian Settipani, Les Ancêtres de Charlemagne (France: Éditions Christian, 1989). Christian Settipani, Continuite Gentilice et Continuite Familiale Dan
2 CONC s Les Familles Senatoriales Romaines, A L'Epoque Imperiale, Mythe et Realite. Linacre, UK: Prosopographica et Genealogica, 2000. ILL. NYPL ASY (Rome) 03-983. Christian Settipani, Continuité gentilice et continuité familiale dans l
2 CONC es familles sénatoriales romaines à l’époque impériale: mythe et réalité, Prosopographica et Genealogica vol. 2 (Linacre College, Oxford, 2000), Addenda et Corrigenda
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Amnius Anicius Julianus (Latin: Amnius Anicius Iulianus; floruit 322–329) was a politician of the Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography: Anicius Julianus was consul in 322 with Petronius Probianus, praefectus urbi Romae between 326 and 329.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is identified with the proconsul of Africa proconsularis who, in 302, received a rescript (an answer to a request for clarification that Julianus had sent) from emperor Diocletian, which ordered the suppression of the Manichees
2 CONC  in Africa, accused of being in contact with the Sasanid empire.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been proposed[2] that the Julianus proconsul of Africa could have been active in 296–297 and that he was put to death by Maximian with fabricated treason charges; his figure of rebel in Africa should be, therefore, linked t
2 CONC o Sabinus Iulianus, an usurper in Africa recorded in Aurelius Victor's Caesares 39.22.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Some recent prosopographical studies[3] have tried to reconstruct his family ties. According to these studies, Julianus, probably born around 270, was son of Sextus Anicius Faustus Paulinus, consul in 298, and younger brother of S
2 CONC extus Anicius Faustus Paulinus, consul in 325. He married Caesonia Manilia (born ca 275), daughter of Lucius Caesonius Ovinius Rufinus Manilius Bassus (or Rufinius), consul in 280; they had Amnius Manius Caesonius Nicomachus Anici
2 CONC us Paulinus Honorius, consul in 334.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes[edit]
2 CONT
