2 CONT Isaac or Sahak of Armenia (338–439) was Catholicos (or Patriarch) of Armenia. He is sometimes known as "Isaac the Great," and as "Սահակ Պարթև / Sahak Parthev" in Armenian, owing to his Parthian origin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac was son of the Christian Saint Narses and descended from the family of Saint Gregory the Illuminator. He was the fifth catholicos of the Arsacid Dynasty of Armenia after St. Gregory I the Enlightener (301–325), St. Aristaces
2 CONC  I (325–333), St. Vrtanes I (333–341) and St. Husik I (341–347). Left an orphan at a very early age, he received an excellent literary education in Constantinople, particularly in the Eastern languages. After his election as patri
2 CONC arch he devoted himself to the religious and scientific training of his people. Armenia was then passing through a grave crisis. In 387 it had lost its independence and been divided between the Byzantine Empire and Persia; each di
2 CONC vision had at its head an Armenian but feudatory king. In the Byzantine territory, however, the Armenians were forbidden the use of the Syriac language, until then exclusively used in divine worship: for this the Greek language wa
2 CONC s to be substituted, and the country gradually hellenized; in the Persian districts, on the contrary, Greek was absolutely prohibited, while Syriac was greatly favoured. In this way the ancient culture of the Armenians was in dang
2 CONC er of disappearing and national unity was seriously compromised.
2 CONT
2 CONT To save both Isaac invented, with the aid of Saint Mesrob, the Armenian alphabet and began to translate the Christian Bible; their translation from the Syriac Peshito was revised by means of the Septuagint, and even, it seems, fro
2 CONC m the Hebrew text (between 410 and 430). The liturgy also, hitherto Syrian was translated into Armenian, drawing at the same time on the liturgy of Saint Basil of Caesarea, so as to obtain for the new service a national colour. Is
2 CONC aac had already established schools for higher education with the aid of disciples whom he had sent to study at Edessa, Melitene, Constantinople, and elsewhere. Through them he now had the principal masterpieces of Greek and Syria
2 CONC n Christian literature translated, e.g. the writings of Athanasius, Cyril of Jerusalem, Basil, the two Gregorys (Gregory of Nazianzus and Gregory of Nyssa), John Chrysostom, Ephrem the Syrian, etc. Armenian literature in its golde
2 CONC n age was, therefore, mainly a borrowed literature.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through Isaac's efforts the churches and monasteries destroyed by the Persians were rebuilt, education was cared for in a generous way, Zoroastrianism which Shah Yazdegerd I tried to set up was cast out, and three councils held to
2 CONC  re-establish ecclesiastical discipline. Isaac is said to have been the author of liturgical hymns.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two letters, written by him to Theodosius II and to Atticus of Constantinople, have been preserved. A third letter addressed to Saint Proclus of Constantinople was not written by him, but dates from the tenth century. Neither did 
2 CONC he have any share, as was wrongly ascribed to him, in the First Council of Ephesus of 431, though, in consequence of disputes which arose in Armenia between the followers of Nestorius and the disciples of Acacius of Melitene and R
2 CONC abbula, Isaac and his church did appeal to Constantinople and through Saint Proclus obtained the desired explanations.
2 CONT
2 CONT A man of enlightened piety and of very austere life, Isaac owed his deposition by the king in 426 to his great independence of character. In 430, he was allowed to resume his patriarchal throne. In his extreme old age he seems to 
2 CONC have withdrawn into solitude, dying at the age of 110. The precise date of his death is not known, but it seems to have occurred between 439 and 441. Hovhannes Draskhanakerttsi says his body was taken to Taron and buried in the vi
2 CONC llage of Ashtishat. Several days are consecrated to his memory in the Armenian Apostolic Church. [edit]  The family of Isaak le Saint SUREN-PAHLAV and ..
2 CONT
2 CONT [128930] SUREN-PAHLAV, Isaak le Saint (Narses le Saint & Sandukht MAMMIKONIDÈS [128929]), patriarch of Armenia (patriarche d'Arménie), born about 351, died 438
2 CONT
