1 NOTE {geni:about_me} She is the matriarch of the Qurayshi tribe.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Bani ''Najjaar'' Tribe. (Part of ''Banu al-Khazrajiyya'' Tribe).
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Najjaar'' translated to '''Carpenter''' in Arabic, indicating family profession of this family/ tribe.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The Banu Najjar were the maternal tribe of the Prophet's grandfather Abdul-Muttalib, they resided in Madinah, and after the Prophet's emigration from Makkah he settled with them in Madinah, and it is at their settlement that the c
2 CONC urrent Mosque of the Prophet stands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not to be confused with Banu Nadir, another Jewish tribe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Salma bint ‘Amr (Arabic: سلمى بنت عمر‎) was the wife of Hashim ibn 'Abd Manaf, thus the great-grandmother of Islamic prophet Muhammad. She was one of the most influential women of the Banu Khazraj tribe and the daughter of ‘Amr of
2 CONC  Banu Najjar clan. She traded and dealt with the caravans on her own behalf.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Banū Hāshim (Hashmi or Hashemi/Syed hashmi) (Arabic: بنو هاشم) is a clan in the Quraysh tribe with a unique maternal bloodline of Israelite ancestry through Salma bint Amr of Banu Najjar. ]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hashim ibn ‘Abd Manaf used to pass through Yathrib (Medina) every year and hold a market at Suq al-Nabt; his was attention caught by Salma's jovial and authoritative manner of trading, and began to make tactful inquiries about her
2 CONC . He soon found out she was well-known and respected, and much sought-after – so much so that she had previously chosen husbands and divorced them as she pleased, and she chose only the best. She was a powerful woman who enjoyed h
2 CONC er own position and tribal prestige, and had no intention of abandoning her home establishment and family group. She remained in her own household, and formed liaisons with those of the men who sought her out that she admired.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of Salma's husbands was the warrior-chief Uhayhah ibn Julah of Banu Jahjaba, a leading celebrity in the tribal fighting of the pre-Islamic period, who possessed one of the largest fortresses in Quba on the outskirts of Yathrib
2 CONC , the Utum ad-Dihyan. Salma had two sons by him, Amr and Mabad. Another of her husbands was her relative Malik ibn Adiy of the Banu Najjar, by whom she had two daughters, Mulaykah and Nuwwar. Yet another was Awf ibn Abdu’l Awf ibn
2 CONC  Abd ibn Harith ibn Zuhrah, by whom she had the daughter Shifa bint Awf.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hashim’s own reputation was such that he did not expect Salma to be anything other than honoured and pleased by his proposal. However, he soon discovered to his chagrin that although she was certainly prepared to consider him, she
2 CONC  would only marry him on her own terms, the chief being that he consented to let her remain in her own home in Yathrib, controlling her own affairs and business entirely on her own as she was used to, not going with him to Makkah 
2 CONC to join his household, and when she bore him a son she kept the boy with her in Yathrib until he was 14 years old or more.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hashim asked her to marry him. She consented on the condition that the control of her affairs should remain entirely in her own hand; and when she bore him a son she kept the boy with her in Yathrib until he was 14 years old or mo
2 CONC re.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hashim accepted, and the wedding took place, with the arrangement that both of them should continue to conduct their lives as before, but Hashim would visit and stay in her house whenever he came to Yathrib, the arrangement suited
2 CONC  both of them. He spent some time with her then he left for As-Sham (present day Syria) again while she was pregnant.
2 CONT
2 CONT Salma gave birth to ‘Abdul-Muttalib in 497 CE and named him Shaiba meaning ‘the ancient one’ or ‘white-haired’ for the streak of white hair amidst his jet-black hair on his head.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Once again, discussions took place. Her husband longed to have their son with him in Makkah as soon as he was weaned, but Salma neither wished to be parted from him, nor for herself to go and live in a polygynous household, so she
2 CONC  insisted that his education should remain her responsibility, and that he should stay in the Yathrib oasis to be bought up in her father's house. Once again, Hashim consented. None of Hashim's family in Makkah learned of his birt
2 CONC h at the time. Shortly after this Salma bore Hashim a second child, a daughter, Ruqaiyyah.[1] Her husband died after falling ill on a journey returning from a business tour to Syria in Gaza, Palestine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her brother-in-law Mutallib went to see Shaiba when he was about eight years old and asked Salma to entrust Shaiba in his care. Salma was unwilling to let her son go and the boy refused to leave his mother without her consent. Mut
2 CONC allib then pointed out that the possibilities Yathrib had to offer were incomparable to Makkah. Salma was impressed with his arguments, so she agreed to let him go.
2 CONT
2 CONT Banū Hāshim (Hashmi or Hashemi/Syed hashmi) (Arabic: بنو هاشم) is a clan in the Quraysh tribe with a unique maternal bloodline of Israelite ancestry through Salma bint Amr of Banu Najjar. This makes Banu Hashim both an Ishmaelite 
2 CONC and Israelite tribe. The Islamic prophet, Muhammad was a member of this Arab tribe; his great-grandfather was Hashim ibn Abd Manaf, for whom the tribe is named. Members of this clan are referred to as Hashemites. Descendants of Mu
2 CONC hammed usually carry the titles Sayyid, Syed or Sharif, or are the Ashraf clan (synonymous to Ahl al-Bayt). Descendants of Muhammed through their mothers side usually carry the title Mirza.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salma_bint_Amr]
2 CONT
2 CONT Salma bint ‘Amr (Arabic: سلمى بنت عمرو‎) was the wife of Hashim ibn 'Abd Manaf, thus the great-grandmother of Islamic prophet Muhammad. Salma bint Amr was Jewish (1).  She was one of the most influential women of the Banu Khazraj 
2 CONC tribe and the daughter of ‘Amr of Banu Najjar clan one of the Jewish tribes in Medina. She traded and dealt with the caravans on her own behalf
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) Reference: [https://www.interfaithny.com/ICLIoct3.php]  THE PROPHET MUHAMMAD AND THE CHILDREN OF ISRAEL BY DR. JOHN ANDREW MORROW
