1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Utbah ibn Rabīʿah prominent leader of the Quraish tribe
2 CONT
2 CONT ʿUtba b. Rabīʿa b. ʿabd s̲h̲ams b. ʿabd manāf, abu ’l-walīd, one of the chiefs of the Meccan tribe of Ḳurays̲h̲, who refused to follow Muḥammad. He met his death in the battle of Badr. His daughter Hind [q.v.] was the wife of Abū 
2 CONC Sufyān [q.v.], and she avenged herself at Uhud on her father’s killer Ḥamza b. ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.
2 CONT
2 CONT S̲h̲ocked by the number of adherents of Muḥammad, ʿUtba, having consulted the other chiefs of the Ḳurays̲h̲, went to the Prophet to offer him anything he would care to ask if he would only abandon his propaganda. According to the 
2 CONC traditional story, Muḥammad in reply only repeated a part of sūra XLI, which made such an impression on him that the effect was still visible when he rejoined his friends, whom he advised not to importune Muḥammad any more. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tradition puts him in a similar light when it represents him as one of those who, on the eve of the battle of Badr, endeavoured in vain to persuade the Ḳurays̲h̲ to withdraw by offering personally to pay blood-money for ʿAmr b. al
2 CONC -Ḥaḍramī, slain by the Muslims. ʿUtba himself was mortally wounded in the battle and his body was thrown into the common ditch (ḳalīb). Muḥammad is said to have thought highly of his gifts, and later tradition is perhaps slightly 
2 CONC more favourable to him than otherwise because he was an ʿAbs̲h̲amī but not an Umawī.
2 CONT
2 CONT (A.J. Wensinck*)
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Ibn His̲h̲ām, Sīra, index
2 CONT
