2 CONT
2 CONT May the rotten apples in our society be guided so we can strive for unity and pave our path and clear all differences and ignorance (ameen)
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Allahumma Salle 'ala Syedina Muhammadin wa 'ala Aalihi Syedina Muhammadin Wa Barik Wa Sallim
2 CONT
2 CONT ==
2 CONT =='''The Messenger of Allah said: "Al-Hasan and al-Husain are the chiefs of the youth of Paradise and Fatimah is the chief of their women."
2 CONT
2 CONT Sunni references:
2 CONT (1) Sahih al-Tirmidhi, v5, p660, on the authority of Abu Sa'id and Hudhayfa (2) Sunan Ibn Majah, Introduction 8 (3) al-Tabarani, on the authorities of: Umar, Ali, Jabir, Abu Hurayrah, Usamah Ibn Zaid, al-Baraa, Ibn 'Adi, and Ibn M
2 CONC asud. (4) al-Kubra, by al-Nisa'i (5) Musnad Ahmad Ibn Hanbal, v1, pp 62,82, v3, pp 3,64, v5, p391 (6) Fada'il al-Sahaba, by Ahmad Hanbal, v2, p771, Tradition #1360 (7) al-Mustadrak, by al-Hakim, v3, pp 166,167 (8) Hilyatul Awliyaa
2 CONC , by Abu Nu'aym, v5, p71 (9) Majma' al-Zawa'id, by al-Haythami, v9, p187 (10) Tuhfatul Ashraf, by Lumzi, v3, p31 (11) Ibn Habban, as mentioned in al-Mawarid, pp 551,553 (12) al-Sawa'iq al-Muhriqah, by Ibn Hajar Haythami, Ch. 11, s
2 CONC ection 3, p290 (13) Mishkat al-Masabih, by Khatib al-Tabrizi, English Version, Tdadition #6154
2 CONT
2 CONT Also it is narrated that:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Messenger of Allah said: "Husain is from me and I am from Husain."
2 CONT
2 CONT Sunni references: (1) Musnad Ahmad Ibn Hanbal, v4, p172 (2) Fadha'il al-Sahaba, by Ahmad Hanbal, v2, p772, Tradition #1361 (3) al-Mustadrak, by al-Hakim, v3, p 177 (4) Amali, by Abu Nu'aym al-Isbahani, p 64 (5) al-Kuna wal Asmaa, 
2 CONC by al-Dulabi, v1, p88 (6) al-Tabarani, v3, p21 (7) Adab by al-Bukhari, also al-Tirmidhi and Ibn Majah, as quoted in: (8) al-Sawa'iq al-Muhriqah, by Ibn Hajar Haythami, Ch. 11, section 3, p291 (9) Mishkat al-Masabih, by Khatib al-T
2 CONC abrizi, English Version, Tdadition #6160
2 CONT
2 CONT The last part of the above tradition probably means that Imam Husain (AS), by sacrificing himself and his family, preserved the religion of Prophet Muhammad (PBUH&HF) from full annihilation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abu Huraira narrated:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Prophet (PBUH) looked toward Ali, Hasan, Husain, and Fatimah (AS), and then said: "I am in war with those who will fight you, and in peace with those who are peaceful to you."
2 CONT
2 CONT Sunni references: (1) Sahih al-Tirmidhi, v5, p699 (2) Sunan Ibn Majah, v1, p52 (3) Fadha'il al-Sahaba, by Ahmad Ibn Hanbal, v2, p767, Tradition #1350 (4) al-Mustadrak, by al-Hakim, v3, p149 (5) Majma' al-Zawa'id, by al-Haythami, v
2 CONC 9, p169 (6) al-Kabir, by al-Tabarani, v3, p30, also in al-Awsat (7) Jami' al-Saghir, by al-Ibani, v2, p17 (8) Tarikh, by al-Khateeb al-Baghdadi, v7, p137 (9) Sawaiq al-Muhriqah, by Ibn Hajar al-Haythami, p144 (10) Talkhis, by al-D
2 CONC hahabi, v3, p149 (11) Dhakha'ir al-Uqba, by al-Muhib al-Tabari, p25 (12) Mishkat al-Masabih, by Khatib al-Tabrizi, English Version, Tdadition #6145
2 CONT
2 CONT Also: The Messenger of Allah said: "He who loves al-Hasan and al-Husain, has loved me, and he who makes them angry has made me angry."
2 CONT
2 CONT Sunni reference: - Sunan Ibn Majah, - al-Mustadrak, by al-Hakim, from Abu Hurairah - Musnad Ahmad Ibn Hanbal, as quited in: - al-Sawa'iq al-Muhriqah, by Ibn Hajar Haythami, Ch. 11, section 3, p292
2 CONT
2 CONT It is narrated in two wordings that:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Messenger of Allah said: "I named Hasan and Husain and Muhsin the names of the sons of Aaron (Haroon) who were: Shubbar, Shubair, and Mushbir."
2 CONT
2 CONT Sunni references: (1) Sunan Abu Dawud al-Tilyasi, v1, p232 (without mentioning Muhsin) (2) Fadha'il al-Sahaba, by Ahmad Ibn Hanbal, v2, p774, Tradition #1365 (3) al-Mustadrak, by al-Hakim, v3, pp 165,168 (4) Kashf al-Astar, by al-
2 CONC Bazzar, v2, p416 (5) Ibn Habban, as quoted in al-Mawarid, p551 (6) al-Tabarani, v3, p100 (7) Idhaah, Abdul Ghani, from Salman al-Farsi (8) al-Mu'jam, by al-Baghawi, as quoted in: (9) al-Sawa'iq al-Muhriqah, by Ibn Hajar Haythami, 
2 CONC Ch. 11, section 3, p292
2 CONT
2 CONT Usamah ibn Zayd narrated:
2 CONT
2 CONT I went to the Prophet (PBUH&HF) one night about something I required and he came out with something (I did not know what) under his cloak. When I had finished telling him my business I asked him what he had under his cloak, and wh
2 CONC en he opened it I saw al-Hasan and al-Husain on his hips. He then said, "These are my sons and my daughter's sons. O Allah, I love them, so I beseech Thee to love them and those who love them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sunni References: - Sahih Tirmidhi, per: - Mishkat al-Masabih, by Khatib al-Tabrizi, English Version, Tdadition #6156
2 CONT
2 CONT Anas ibn Malik Narrated: When Allah's Messenger (PBUH&HF) was asked which member of his family was the dearest to him, he replied, "Al-Hasan and al-Husain." He used to say to Fatimah, "Call my two sons to me," and then would sniff
2 CONC  and cuddle them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sunni References: - Sahih Tirmidhi, per: - Mishkat al-Masabih, by Khatib al-Tabrizi, English Version, Tdadition #6158
2 CONT
2 CONT Imam Husain (AS) said: "Don't you see that the truth is not followed and the falsehood is not discouraged? (The situation is so severe) so that a Believer wishes to meet Allah (i.e., to die). And today I don't see death but prospe
2 CONC rity, and living with tyrants is nothing but disgust and disgrace."
2 CONT
2 CONT Sunni Reference: Hilyatul Awliyaa, by Abu Nu'aym, v2, p39
2 CONT
2 CONT Imam Husain (AS) said in the day of Ashura: "If you do not have any religion, then at least be noble and broad-minded in your present life."
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://www.shiarightpath.com
2 CONT
2 CONT In the month of Muharram 61 AH (approx. 20 October 680 AD), an event took place in Iraq at a place known as Kerbala on the bank of the river Euphrates. It seemed in those days insignificant from the historical point of view. A lar
2 CONC ge army which had been mobilised by the Umayyad regime besieged a group of persons numbering less than a hundred and put them under pressure to pay allegiance to the Caliph of the time and submit to his authority. The small group 
2 CONC resisted and a severe battle took place in which they were all killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT It appeared at that time that like hundreds of similar events, this battle would be recorded in history and forgotten in time. However, the events that occurred on the 10th day of Muharram in Kerbala were to become a beacon and an
2 CONC  inspiration for future generations. In this article, we shall examine briefly the principal adversaries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Who is Hussain?
2 CONT
2 CONT The leader of the small band of men who were martyred in Kerbala was none other than Husain (A), son of Ali bin Abi Talib (A) and grandson of the Holy Prophet (S). Who was Husain? He was the son of Fatima (A) for whom the Holy Pro
2 CONC phet (S) said, "Husain is from me and I am from Husain. May God love whoever loves Husain." [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT With the passing away of his brother Hasan(A) in 50 AH, Husain (A) became the leader of the household of the Holy Prophet (S). He respected the agreement of peace signed by Hasan (A) and Muawiya, and, despite the urging of his fol
2 CONC lowers, he did not undertake any activity that threatened the political status quo. Rather he continued with the responsibility of looking after the religious needs of the people and was recognised for his knowledge, piety and gen
2 CONC erosity. An example of the depth of his perception can be seen in his beautiful du'a on the day of Arafat, wherein he begins by explaining the qualities of Allah, saying:
2 CONT
2 CONT " (Oh Allah) How could an argument be given about Your Existence by a being whose total and complete existence is in need of you? When did you ever disappear so that you might need an evidence and logic to lead (the people) toward
2 CONC s You? And when did You ever become away and distant so that your signs and effects made the people get in touch with you? Blind be the eye which does not see You (whereas) You are observing him. What did the one who missed You fi
2 CONC nd? And what does the one who finds You lack? Certainly, the one who got pleased and inclined toward other than You, came to nothingness (failed)."
2 CONT
2 CONT On the other hand, we have Yazid, whose father (Muawiya) and grandfather (Abu Sufyan - the arch-enemy of the Prophet) had always tried to sabotage the mission of the Holy Prophet, and who showed his true colour by stating in a poe
2 CONC m, "Bani Hashim had staged a play to obtain kingdom, there was neither any news from God nor any revelation." [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mas'udi writes that Yazid was a pleasure-seeking person, given to wine drinking and playing with pets. It is no wonder that Husain's response to Yazid's governor, when asked to pay allegiance to Yazid was, "We are the household of
2 CONC  the prophethood, the source of messengership, the descending-place of the angels, through us Allah had began (showering His favours) and with us He has perfected (His favours), whereas Yazid is a sinful person, a drunkard, the ki
2 CONC ller of innocent people and one who openly indulges in sinful acts. A person like me can never pledge allegiance to a person like him ..." [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT The revolution of Husain (A) was an Islamic movement spearheaded by one of the great leaders of Islam. The principles and laws of Islam demanded that Husain (A) act to warn the Ummah of the evil situation which it was in, and to s
2 CONC tand in the way of the deviating ruler. As Husain (A) himself remarked when he left Madina for the last time, "I am not rising (against Yazid) as an insolent or an arrogant person, or a mischief-monger or tyrant. I have risen (aga
2 CONC inst Yazid) as I seek to reform the Ummah of my grandfather. I wish to bid the good and forbid the evil." [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hussain (A) was killed on the battlefield as he did Sajdah. His head was removed from his body on the plains of Kerbala, mounted on a spear, and paraded through villages and towns as it was taken to Damascus and presented at the f
2 CONC eet of Yazid.
2 CONT
2 CONT Why remember Ashura?
2 CONT
2 CONT Why is Husain (A) regarded as the "leader of the martyrs" ? It is because he was not just the victim of an ambitious ruler. There is no doubt that the tragedy of Kerbala, when ascribed to the killers, is a criminal and terrible ac
2 CONC t. However when ascribed to Husain (A) himself, it represents a conscious confrontation and a courageous resistance for a sacred cause. The whole nation had failed to stand up to Yazid. They had succumbed to his will, and deviatio
2 CONC n and regression towards the pre-Islamic ways were increasing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Passiveness by Husain (A) in this situation would have meant the end of Islam as we know it. Thus Husain (A) took upon himself the responsibility of the whole nation. The greatest tragedy was that one who stood up for the noblest 
2 CONC of causes, the defence of Islam, was cut down in so cruel a manner.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is for this reason that the sacrifice of Husain (A) is commemorated annually throughout the Muslim world. Our sorrow never abates as we relive the tragedy. As Allama Iqbal says in his Baqiyat (in Urdu):
2 CONT
2 CONT Ronay wala hoon Shaheed-e-Kerbala key gham men main, Kya durey maqsad na dengey Saqiye Kausar mujhey
2 CONT
2 CONT I am one who weeps at the plight of the Martyr of Kerbala Won't the reward be given to me by the Keeper of Kauser (Imam Ali (A))
2 CONT
2 CONT The commemoration of Ashura on the 10th of Muharram every year serves to remind us of the sacrifices of the family of the Prophet (S). It also makes us aware of the people, then and now, who tried to destroy Islam and the family o
2 CONC f the Prophet (S) and all that they stood for - as well as those who watched, listened and did nothing.
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT [1] Ibn Majah: Sunan, Hadith 144. [2] Ibn Jarir: Tarikhu'l Umam wa'l Muluk, vol.13, p.2174. [3] Sayyid ibn Ta'us: Maqtalu'l Husain, pp.10-11 [4] Al-Khatid al-Khuwarazmi: Maqtalu'l Husain ,vol.1, p.88.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://www.ansarkarbala.com
2 CONT
2 CONT Taken from Kitab-al Irshad by Shaykh Al-Mufid Imam Hussain (as) sets out on his Journey
2 CONT
2 CONT Al Husyan, the blessings of God be on him, set out from Mecca to Iraq on the day of Muslim's (attempted) rising in Kufa, that is the day of Tarwiya, after staying in Mecca for the rest of Shaban, the month of Rmadhan, Shawwal and 
2 CONC Dhu al Qada and eight days of Dhu- al-Hijja in the year 60 A.H. (680). During his stay in Mecca, peace be on him, a number of Hijazis and Basrans had gathered around him, joining themselves to his household and his retainers (mawa
2 CONC li).
2 CONT
2 CONT When he determined on journeying to Iraq, he made the circumbulation of the (sacred) House and the ritual running between al-Safa and al-Marwa. Then he left the state of consecration (for the pilgrimage) (after) he had performed t
2 CONC he lesser pilgrimage (umra) because he was not able to perform the greater pilgrimage (hajj). Through fear of being apprehended in Mecca, and being taken to Yazid b. Muiawiya, he, peace be on him, had set out early with his House,
2 CONC  his sons and those of his Shia who had joined him.
2 CONT
2 CONT [As it has been reported to us:]
2 CONT
2 CONT News of Muslim's (capture and death) had not yet reached him because (it had only happened) on the day he set out.
2 CONT
2 CONT [It is reported that al-Farazdaq, the poet, said:]
2 CONT
2 CONT I made the pilgrimage with my mother in the year 60 A.H. (680). I was driving her camel when I entered the sanctuary. (There) I met al- Husayn b. Ali, peace be on them, leaving Mecca accompanied by (some men carrying) swords and s
2 CONC hields.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Whose caravan is this?" I asked.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Al-Husayn b. Ali's, peace be on them," was the reply. So I went up and greeted him.
2 CONT
2 CONT "May God grant you your request and (fulfil) your hope in what you want, by my father and mother, son of the Apostle of God," I said to him. "But what is making you hurry away from the pilgrimage?"
2 CONT
2 CONT "If I did not hurry away, I would be apprehended," he replied. Then he asked me: "Who are you?"
2 CONT
2 CONT "An Arab," I answered and he did not question me (about myself) any further.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Tell me about the people you have left behind you," he asked.
2 CONT
2 CONT "You have asked a good (question)," I answered. "The hearts of the people are with you but their swords are against you. The decision comes from Heaven and God does what he wishes."
2 CONT
2 CONT "You have spoken truly of the affair belonging to God," he replied.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Every day He (is involved) in (every) matter" (LV, 29) If fate sends down what we like and are pleased with, we praise God for His blessings. He is the One from Whom help should be sought in order to give thanks to Him. However, 
2 CONC although fate may frustrate (our) hopes, yet He does not destroy (the souls of) those whose intention is the truth and whose hearts are pious."
2 CONT
2 CONT "True, God brings you what you wish for (ultimately) and guards you against what you are threatened by," I said. Then I asked him about matters concerning vows and pilgrimage rites. He told me about them and then moved his mount o
2 CONC ff, saying farewell, and so we parted.
2 CONT
2 CONT When al-Husayn b. Ali, peace be on them, left Mecca, Yahya b. Said b. al-'As met him with a group (of men). They had been sent to him by 'Amr b. Said.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Come back from where you are going," they ordered. But he refused (to obey) them and continued. The two groups came to blows and hit at each other with whips. However al-Husayn and his followers resisted fiercely. Al-Husayn conti
2 CONC nued until he got to al- Tanim. There he met a camel-train which had come from Yemen. He hired from its people (additional) camels for himself and his followers to ride.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then he said to the owners (of the camels): "Whoever (of you) wants to come with us to Iraq, we will pay his hire and enjoy his company and whoever wants to leave some way along the road we will pay his hire for the distance he ha
2 CONC s travelled."
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of the people went with him but others refused. Abd Allah b. Jafar sent his sons, Awn and Muhammad, after him, and he wrote a letter to him which he gave to them. In it, he said:
2 CONT
2 CONT  I ask you before God (to return) if you have set out when you
2 CONT  see my letter. For I am very concerned because the direction in
2 CONT  which you are heading will have within it your destruction, and
2 CONT  the extirpation of your House. If you are destroyed today, the
2 CONT  light of the land will be extinguished; for you are the (standard)
2 CONT  of those who are rightly-guided and the hope of the believers.
2 CONT  Do not hurry on your journey as I am following this letter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Greetings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abd Allah, then went to 'Amr b. Sad and asked him to write to al-Husayn (offering him) a guarantee of security, and (promising) to favour him, so that he would return from where he was going. Amr b. Said wrote a letter in which he
2 CONC  offered him favour and a guarantee of security for himself. He dispatched it with his brother Yahya b. Said. Yahya b. Said went after him (as did) Abd Allah after dispatching his sons. The two handed ('Amr's) letter to him and st
2 CONC rove (to persuade) him to return.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I have seen the Apostle of God, may God bless him and his family, in my sleep," answered (al-Husayn), "and he ordered me (to do) what I am carrying out."
2 CONT
2 CONT "What was that vision?" they both asked.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I have not told anyone of it," he answered, "and I am not going to tell anyone until I meet my Lord, the Mighty and Exalted."
2 CONT
2 CONT When 'Abd Allah b. Ja'far despaired of (persuading) him, he told his sons, Awn and Muhammad, to stay with him, to go with him and to struggle on behalf of him. He returned with Yahya b. Sa'id to Mecca.
2 CONT
2 CONT Al Husayn, peace be on him, pressed on swiftly and directly towards Iraq until he reached Dhat' Irq.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Ubayd Allah b. Ziyad had learnt of the journey of al- Husayn, peace be on him, from Mecca to Kufa, he had sent al- Husayn b. Numayr, the commander of the bodyguard (shurta), to station himself at al-Qadisiyya and to set up a 
2 CONC (protective) link of cavalry between the area of al-Qadisiyya to Khaffan and the area of al-Qadisiyya to al-Qutqutaniyya. He informed the men that al- Husayn was heading for Iraq.
2 CONT
2 CONT When al-Husayn, peace be on him, reached al-Hajiz (a hill above) Batn al-Rumma, he sent Qays b. Mushir al Saydawi - some say it was his brother-in-nurture, Abd Allah b. Yuqtur to Kufa. For he, peace be upon him, had not yet learnt
2 CONC  the news of (the fate of) Ibn 'Aqil. He sent a letter with him:
2 CONT
2 CONT  In the Name of God, the Merciful, the Compassionate
2 CONT  From al-Husayn b. Ali
2 CONT  To his brother believers and Muslims,
2 CONT
2 CONT Greetings to you, I praise God before you, other than Whom
2 CONT  there is no deity. Muslim b. Aqil's letter came to me, informing
2 CONT  me of your sound judgement and the agreement of your leaders
2 CONT  to support us, and to seek our rights. I have asked God to make
2 CONT  your actions good and reward you with the greatest reward. I
2 CONT  set out to you from Mecca on 8th of Dhu al-Hijja, the Day of
2 CONT  Tarwiya. When my messenger reaches you, be urgent and
2 CONT  purposeful in your affiars, for I am coming to you within the
2 CONT  (next few) days.
2 CONT
2 CONT Greeting and the mercy and blessings of God.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muslim had written to al-Husayn seventeen days before he was killed and the Kufans had written to him: "Here you have a hundred thousand swords. Do not delay."
2 CONT
2 CONT Qays b. Mushir went towards Kufa with the letter. However, when he reached al-Qadisiyya, al-Husayn b. Numayr apprehended him and sent him to Ubayd Allah b. Ziyad.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Go up on the pulpit," Ubayd Allah b. Ziyad ordered him, "and curse the liar, al-Husayn b. Ali, peace be on him"
2 CONT
2 CONT Qays went up on the pulpit and praised and glorified God. Then he said:
2 CONT
2 CONT People, this man, al-Husayn b. 'Ali the best of God's
2 CONT  creatures, the son of Fatima, the daughter of the Apostle, may God bless him and his family and grant them peace, (is nearby). I am his messenger to you. Answer him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then he cursed Ubayd Allah b. Ziyad and his father and prayed for forgiveness for Ali b. Abi Talib and blessed him. 'Ubayd Allah ordered him to be thrown from the top of the palace. They threw him and he was smashed to pieces.
2 CONT
2 CONT Taken from Kitab-al Irshad by Shaykh Al-Mufid The Continuation of the Journey
2 CONT
2 CONT (While this had been going on) al-Husayn, peace be on him, had left Hajiz in the direction of Kufa until he came to one of the watering (places) of the Arabs. There there was 'Abd Allah b. Muti al-'Adawli, who was staying there. W
2 CONC hen he saw al-Husayn, peace be on him, he got up and said to him: "(May I ransom) my father and mother for you, son of the Apostle of God, what has brought you (here)?" He brought him (forward) and helped him to dismount.
2 CONT
2 CONT "It is a result of the death of Muawiya as you would know," replied al Husayn, peace be on him. "The Iraqis have written to me urging me to (come to) them"
2 CONT
2 CONT "I remind you, son of the Apostle of God, (of God) and the sacredness of Islam, lest it be violated. I adjure you before God (to think) about the sacredness of Quraysh. I adjure you before God (to think) about the sacredness of th
2 CONC e Arabs. By God, if you seek that which is in the hands of Banu Umayya, they will kill you. If they kill you, they will never fear anyone after you. Then it will be the sacredness of Islam which is violated, and the sacredness of 
2 CONC Quraysh and the sacredness of the Arabs. Don't do it! Don't go to Kufa! Don't expose yourself to Banu Umayya!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Husayn, peace be on him, insisted on continuing his journey. (In the meantime) 'Ubayd Allah b. Ziyad had ordered (the area) which was between Waqisa and the roads to Syria and Basra to be occupied (so that) they should not let 
2 CONC anyone enter, nor anyone leave (Kufa).
2 CONT
2 CONT However, al-Husayn, peace be on him, went on without knowing anything (of that) until he met some Arabs. He asked them (about the situation) and they told him: "No, by God, we don't know (anything about it) except that we cannot g
2 CONC et into or out of (Kufa)."
2 CONT
2 CONT He continued on his journey.
2 CONT
2 CONT [A group of Fazara and Bajila reported (the following account). They said:]
2 CONT
2 CONT We were with Zuhayr b. al-Qayn al-Bajah when we came from Mecca. (Although) we were travelling alongside al Husayn, peace be on him, there was nothing more hateful to us than that we should stop with him at a halting place. (Yet) 
2 CONC when al-Husayn, peace be on him, travelled and halted, we could not avoid halting with him. Al-Husayn halted at the side (of the road) and we halted at the (other) side (of the road). While we were sitting, eating our food, a mess
2 CONC enger of al- Husayn, peace be on him, approached, greeted us and entered (our camp).
2 CONT
2 CONT "Zuhayr b. al-Qayn," he said, "Abu 'Abd Allah al-Husayn, peace be on him, has sent me to you (to ask) you to come to him."
2 CONT
2 CONT Each man of us threw away what was in his hands (i.e. threw up his hands in horror); it was (as surprising) as if birds had alighted on our heads.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Glory be to God," (Zuhayr's) wife said to him, "did the son of the Messenger of God send for you? Then aren't you going to him? If you went to him, you would hear what he had to say. Then you could leave him (if you wanted to)."
2 CONT
2 CONT Zuhayr b. al-Qayn went (across) to him. It was not long before he returned to announce that he was heading east. He ordered his tent (to be struck) and (called for) his luggage, mounts and equipment. His tent was pulled down and t
2 CONC aken to al-Husayn, peace be on him, then he said to his wife: "You are divorced, go back to your family, for I do not want anything to befall you except good."
2 CONT
2 CONT Then he said to his companions:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Whoever wants to follow me (may do so), otherwise he is at the end of his covenant with me (i.e. released from obedience to follow Zuhayr as the leader of his tribal group). I will tell you a story (of something which happened to
2 CONC  me once): we were raiding a rich land. God granted us victory and we won (a lot of) booty. Salman al-Farsl, the mercy of God be on him, said to us: 'Are you happy with the victory which God has granted you and the booty you have 
2 CONC won?' We said: 'Yes.' Then he said: 'Therefore when you meet the lord of the young men of the family of Muhammad be happier to fight with them than you are with the booty which you have obtained today.' As for me. I pray that God 
2 CONC may be with you."
2 CONT
2 CONT He remained among the people with al-Husayn until he was killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Abd Allah b. Sulayman and al-Mundhir b. Mushamill both from Asad, reported:]
2 CONT
2 CONT When we had finished the pilgrimage, there was no concern more important to us than to join al-Husayn, peace be on him, on the road, so that we might see what happened in his affair. We went along trotting our two camels speedily 
2 CONC until we joined him at Zarud. As we approached, there we (saw) a man from Kufa who had changed his route when he had seen al-Husayn, peace be on him. Al-Husayn, peace be on him, had stopped as if he wanted (to speak to) him, but (
2 CONC the man) ignored him and went on. We went on towards the man. One of us said to the other: "Come with us to ask this man if he has news of Kufa."
2 CONT
2 CONT We came up to him and greeted him. He returned out greeting.
2 CONT
2 CONT "From which (tribe) do you come, fellow?" we asked.
2 CONT
2 CONT "(I am) an Asadi," he answered.
2 CONT
2 CONT "We also are Asadis," we said. "Who are you?"
2 CONT
2 CONT "I am Bakr b. so and so," he answered and we told him our lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Tell us of the people (you have left) behind you?" we asked.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Yes," he replied, "I only left Kufa after Muslim b. 'Aqil and Hani' b. 'Urwa had been killed. I saw them being dragged by their legs into the market-place."
2 CONT
2 CONT We went on to join al-Husayn, peace be on him, and we were travelling close to him until he stopped at al-Thalabiyya in the evening. We caught up with him when he stopped and we greeted him. He returned our greeting.
2 CONT
2 CONT "May God have mercy on you," we said, "we have news. If you wish, we will tell it to you publicly or if you wish, secretly."
2 CONT
2 CONT He looked at us and at his followers.
2 CONT
2 CONT "There is no veil for these men," he answered.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Did you see the rider whom you were near, yesterday evening?"
2 CONT
2 CONT "Yes," he answered, "I had wanted to question him."
2 CONT
2 CONT "We have got the news from him and spared you (the trouble of) questioning him," we said. "He was a man from our (tribe), of sound judgment, honesty and intelligence. He told us that he had only left Kufa after Muslim and Hani' ha
2 CONC d been killed, and he had seen them being dragged by their legs into the market-place."
2 CONT
2 CONT "We belong to God and to Him we shall return; may God have mercy on them both," said al-Husayn, and he repeated that several times.
2 CONT
2 CONT "We adjure you before God," we exhorted him, "for your own life and for your House that you do not go from this place, for you have no one to support you in Kufa and no Shia. Indeed we fear that such men (will be the very ones who
2 CONC ) will be against you."
2 CONT
2 CONT "What is your opinion," he asked, looking towards the sons of 'Aqil, "now that Muslim has been killed?"
2 CONT
2 CONT "By God," they declared, "we will not go back until we have taken our vengeance or have tasted (the death) which he tasted."
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Husayn, peace be on him, came near us and said: "There is nothing good (left) in life for these men."
2 CONT
2 CONT Then we knew that his decision had been taken to continue the journey.
2 CONT
2 CONT "May God be good to you," we said.
2 CONT
2 CONT "May God have mercy on you both," he answered.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then his followers said to him: "By God, you are not the same as Muslim b. Aqil. If you go to Kufa, the people will rush to (support) you."
2 CONT
2 CONT He was silent and waited until daybreak. Then he ordered his boys and servants to get a lot of water, to give (the people) to drink and more for the journey. They set out (once more) and went on to Zubala. News of eAbd Allah b. Yu
2 CONC qtur reached him. He took out a written statement to the people and read it to them:
2 CONT
2 CONT  In the name of God, the Merciful, the Compassionate,
2 CONT  News of the dreadful murder of Muslim b. Aqil Hani' b.
2 CONT  Urwa, and Abd Allah b. Yuqtur has reached us. Our Shia
2 CONT  have deserted us . Those of you who would prefer to leave us, may leave freely without guilt.
2 CONT
2 CONT The people began to disperse from him to right and left until there were only left with him those followers who had come with him from Medina, and a small group of those who had joined him. Al-Husayn had done that because he reali
2 CONC sed that the Arabs who had followed him had only followed him because they thought that he was going to a land where the inhabitants' obedience to him had already been established. And he did not want them to accompany him without
2 CONC  being (fully) aware of what they were going to.
2 CONT
2 CONT At dawn, he ordered his followers to provide themselves with water and with extra (supplies of it). Then they set out until they passed Batn al Aqaba. He stopped there and was met by a shaykh of the Banu Ikrima called Amr b. Lawdh
2 CONC an.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Where are you headings." he asked.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Kufa," replied al-Husayn, peace be on him.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I implore you before God," exhorted the shaykh, "why are you going there? You won't come to anything there except the points of spears and the edges of swords. If those who sent for you were enough to support you in battle and ha
2 CONC d prepared the ground for you, and you came to them, that would be a wise decision. However, in the light of the situation as it has been described I don't think that you ought to do it."'''====
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT troisieme Emam chiite
2 CONT
2 CONT HOSAYN B. ʿALI B. ABI ṬĀLEB, ABU ʿABD-ALLĀH (also referred to among Shiʿites as Sayyed-al-Šohadāʾ), the second surviving grandson of the Prophet Moḥammad through his daughter Fāṭema (q.v.) and the third Imam of the Shiʿites after 
2 CONC his father and his elder brother Ḥasan. http://bit.ly/1Nkq0eU
2 CONT
2 CONT Lorsqu'advient le nouveau calife omeyyade Yazīd lahine, fils de Muawiya, Hussein refuse de lui prêter allégeance. Pour éviter la mort, il fuit Damas et se réfugie à La Mecque. Or, le gouverneur du calife Yazid, Ibn Ziyâd, fait tue
2 CONC r l'émissaire que Hussein lui a envoyé à Kûfa. Peu après, Hussein se met en route pour cette ville, « imprudemment » selon certaines sources, en toute connaissance de cause afin de dévoiler aux yeux des musulmans l'injustice des O
2 CONC meyyades selon d'autres. Il aurait ainsi rencontré en chemin le poète Abu Firas Hammam dit « al Farazdak » qui l'aurait prévenu : « Ô Hussein, leurs cœurs sont avec toi mais leurs épées sont sorties de leur fourreaux ! »
2 CONT
2 CONT Hussein ibn Ali dans une lettre adressée à son frère Muhammad Ibn al-Hanafiyyah ainsi que dans d'autres occasions, évoque les raisons de son départ de Médine, de son refus du pouvoir de Yazid, et de sa révolution contre lui. Il y 
2 CONC explique le sens de son mouvement et les fondements de sa confrontation avec le nouveau régime Omayyade:«Je ne me suis pas soulevé de gaieté de coeur, ni pour une quelconque insatisfaction personnelle, ni par subversion ni injuste
2 CONC ment. Je me suis soulevé pour réformer la Umma de mon grand-père, le Messager de Dieu, pour commander le bien et interdire le mal, et pour suivre les traces de mon grand-père et de mon père ... » 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mosquée Al-Husayn au Caire Escorté d'une petite troupe de 72 personnes, dont les membres de sa propre famille, il part rejoindre ses partisans de Kûfa qui l'avaient appelé à l'aide et lui avaient promis obéissance5. Ibn Ziyâd inte
2 CONC rcepte cette troupe à Kerbala et exige qu'Hussein prête allégeance au calife Yâzid. Devant le refus de celui-ci, une bataille très inégale s'engage, le 10 muharram, mois sacré du calendrier musulman. Tous les compagnons de Hussein
2 CONC  sont tués (10 octobre 680 ; 10 muharram 61AH). Le combat semble perdu d'avance, la petite armée de Hussein ne pouvant se mesurer à 30 000 adversaires, et ce d'autant plus que l'armée omeyyade contrôle tous les accès à l'eau. Ils 
2 CONC sont donc obligés de combattre durant deux journées sous un soleil de plomb et sans eau aucune, avec un siège de 10 jours au total.
2 CONT
2 CONT La soif et les durs combats ont raison d'eux. Seul le plus jeune fils d'Hussein, 'Alî Zayn al-'Âbidîn, est épargné avec les femmes et sa sœur Zaynab fille d'Ali. Le cadavre d'Hussein est enterré sur place et sa tête décapitée est 
2 CONC envoyée à Damas au calife Yâzid, ainsi que les survivants devenus captifs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lorsqu’on apporte la tête décapitée de l'imam Hussein au calife Yazîd Ier, celui-ci est effrayé6.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://bit.ly/1WKoihq
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a grandson of Muhammad and brother of Hasan ibn Ali. Husayn opposed the validity of Caliph Yazid I. As a result, he and his family were later killed in the Battle of Karbala by Yazid's forces. After this incident, the comme
2 CONC moration of Husayn ibn Ali has become a central ritual in Shia identity
2 CONT
2 CONT Husayn ibn ‘Alī ibn Abī Tālib (Arabic: الحسين بن علي بن أبي طالب‎‎; 8 January 626 – 10 October 680) (3rd/4th Sha'aban 4 AH – 10th Muharram 61 AH), also spelled as Husain, Hussain or Hussein, was the son of Ali ibn Abi Ṭalib (fourt
2 CONC h Rashidun Caliph of Sunni Islam, and first Imam of Shia Islam) and Fatimah Zahra (daughter of Muhammad) and the younger brother of Hasan ibn Ali. Husayn is an important figure in Islam, as he is a member of the Ahl al-Bayt (the h
2 CONC ousehold of Muhammad) and Ahl al-Kisa, as well as being the third Shia Imam. Hussain became the Shia Imam and the head of Banu Hashim after death of his older brother, Hasan ibn Ali, in 670 (50 AH). His father's supporters (Shi'a 
2 CONC Ali) in Kufah gave their allegiance to him. However, he told he still bound to the peace treaty between Hasan and Muawiyah I and they should wait until Muawiyah was alive. Later, Hussain did not accept the request of Muawiyah for 
2 CONC the succession of his son, Yazid I, and considered this action as the breach of the Hasan–Muawiya treaty.[6] When Muawiyah I died in 680, Husayn refused to pledge allegiance to Yazid I, who had just appointed as Umayyad caliph by 
2 CONC Muawiyah, because he considered Umayyads as oppressive and religiously misguided regime. He insisted on his legitimacy based on his own special position as a direct descendant of Muhammad and his legitimate legatees. As a conseque
2 CONC nce, he left Medina, his home town, to take refuge in Mecca in 60 AH.[6][7] There, the people of Kufa sent letters to him, asking his help and pledging their allegiance to him. So he traveled towards Kufa.[6] At a place near Kufa,
2 CONC  known as Karbala, his caravan was intercepted by Yazid I's army. He was killed and beheaded in the Battle of Karbala on 10 October 680 (10 Moḥarram 61) by Shimr Ibn Thil-Jawshan, along with most of his family and companions.[8] A
2 CONC nger at Husayn's death was turned into a rallying cry that helped undermine the Umayyad Caliphate legitimacy and ultimately overthrow the Umayyad Caliphate by Abbasid Revolution.[9][10]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''2nd Fatimid Imaam''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT * ''Imaamate Period/ Reign: 12 years; 49-61 AH (669-680 AD)''
2 CONT * Wilaadat (Birth): 
2 CONT * Wafaat (Death):  10th Muharram ul-Haraam 61 AH (12th October 680 AD)
