2 CONT ID: I11601
2 CONT
2 CONT Name: Lucinia Eudoxia of Constantinople
2 CONT *Given Name: Lucinia Eudoxia
2 CONT *Surname: of Constantinople
2 CONT *Sex: F
2 CONT
2 CONT _UID: D53E33CA201FD811BE490080C8C142CC56FE
2 CONT *Change Date: 18 Jan 2004
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: ABT 422 in Constantinople, Byzantine Empire
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 460
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Theodosius II of Eastern Roman Empire b: 10 APR 401 in Constantinople, Byzantine Empire 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Eudocia of Athens b: ABT 401 in Athens, Greece
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Flavius Valentinian III of Rome b: 2 JUL 419 in Ravenna, Italy
2 CONT *Married: 29 OCT 437 in Constantinople, Byzantine Empire
2 CONT *Children
2 CONT **1.  Lucina Eudoxia b: 440
2 CONT **2.  Placidia of Rome b: ABT 440
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Licinia Eudoxia was born in 422, the daughter of the eastern emperor Theodosius II (408-450) and Aelia Eudoxia. In 424 she was betrothed to the western emperor Valentinian III (425-455) , and the marriage was performed in Constant
2 CONC inople in 437. She bore two children, Eudocia and Placidia. She received the title Augusta in 439. After Valentinian's murder at Rome in 455, she was compelled to marry his successor Petronius Maximus (455) , and it later was clai
2 CONC med that it was she who invited the Vandal Geiseric to Rome in the same year. After the ensuing sack, she and her two daughters were carried back to Carthage. It was not until the early 460s that she and Placidia were set free, an
2 CONC d withdrew to Constantinople, where she spent the remainder of her years. Eudocia remained in Africa as the wife of Geiseric's son Huneric.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The middle of the Fifth Century was a time of great crisis for the dwindling remnant of the Western Roman Empire. What used to be the most powerful empire on Earth was just a small European state by A. D. 437, when Valentinian mar
2 CONC ried Licinia Eudoxia, a distant relative. The Eastern Roman Empire was still a large and powerful one, ruling the lands from Illyricum (modern day Serbia, Bosnia, and Herzegovina) to Mesopotamia and Egypt. The Western Empire claim
2 CONC ed only Italy and a small part of Gaul (modern France) by this time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Licinia Eudoxia was the great granddaughter of Theodosius I, the last emperor to rule over both eastern and western halves of the Roman Empire. Valentinian was the grandson of Theodosius I. Marriage between distant relatives of th
2 CONC e imperial family had by this time become commonplace. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After Valentinian III was murdered in 455, Petronius Maximus was elevated to the imperial throne. It was widely believed that he had had something to do with Valentinian's murder, but he was a very wealthy and powerful senator. He
2 CONC  forced Valentinian's widow to marry him, and her daughter to marry his son. It is believed that she appealed to Gaiseric, the Vandal ruler of Africa for help. At any rate, that is the excuse Gaiseric offered for the deed he now s
2 CONC et out to do. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaiseric turned his eyes toward the great wealth that remained in the city of Rome. Lacking an adequate army to protect herself, the Eternal City was a tempting target for a raiding warlord or pirate with only modest means. Gambli
2 CONC ng that Theodosius II in the East had had not the will nor the ability to defend the Western lands, Gaiseric launched his navy, led largely by pirate captains, and attacked Rome. The citizens of the city panicked when news reached
2 CONC  them of the impending invasion. The people and their emperor Petronius Maximus fled. The cowardly emperor was killed by his own frightened and dismayed subjects while attempting to escape the doomed city. Gaiseric found little or
2 CONC ganized resistance and easily entered the city. He plundered the helpless city and its remaining inhabitants for two weeks, his often drunken troops burning, raping and pillaging at will. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Three of the most valuable prizes captured from the devastated city were Licinia Eudoxia and her two daughters. The eldest daughter, Eudoxia, was forced to marry Gaiseric's son Huneric. The three imperial ladies remained captives 
2 CONC in Gaiseric's household until the Eastern emperor Leo was able to get Gaiseric to give the ladies up to him sometime in the 460's. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Licinia Eudoxia made her home in the Eastern capital of Constantinople after her release and the events of the rest of her life are lost to history
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Licinius Eudoxia was the daughter of the powerful Eastern Emperor Theodosius II, married off to the Western Emperors Valentinian III and his successor Petronius Maximus
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Licinia Eudoxia
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph W. Mathisen
2 CONT
2 CONT University of South Carolina
2 CONT
2 CONT Licinia Eudoxia was born in 422, the daughter of the eastern emperor Theodosius II (408-450) and Aelia Eudoxia. In 424 she was betrothed to the western emperor Valentinian III (425-455) , and the marriage was performed in Constant
2 CONC inople in 437. She bore two children, Eudocia and Placidia. She received the title Augusta in 439. After Valentinian's murder at Rome in 455, she was compelled to marry his successor Petronius Maximus (455) , and it later was clai
2 CONC med that it was she who invited the Vandal Geiseric to Rome in the same year. After the ensuing sack, she and her two daughters were carried back to Carthage. It was not until the early 460s that she and Placidia were set free, an
2 CONC d withdrew to Constantinople, where she spent the remainder of her years. Eudocia remained in Africa as the wife of Geiseric's son Huneric.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Critical Studies:
2 CONT
2 CONT De Salis, J.F.W. "The Coins of the Two Eudoxias, Eudocia, Placidia, and Honoria, and of Theodosius II, Marcian, and Leo I, Struck in Italy." Numismatic Chronicle 7(1867): 203-215.
2 CONT
2 CONT Duckett, Eleanor Shipley. Medieval Portraits from the East and West. Ann Arbor, 1972.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clover, Frank M. "The Family and Early Career of Anicius Olybrius." Historia 27(1978): 169-196.
2 CONT
2 CONT Copyright (C) 1996, Ralph W. Mathisen. This file may be copied on the condition that the entire contents,including the header and this copyright notice, remain intact.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Licinia was at one time had children with at least three men - Flavius Valentinian of Rome III, Gensreic (Spear King) Vandals, and Petronius Maximus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Licinius Eudoxia was the daughter of the powerful Eastern Emperor Theodosius II, married off to the Western Emperors Valentinian III and his successor Petronius Maximus.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Her ancestry is found elasewhere on the tree - Licinia's father was Theodosus II and her mother was Athenais Eudocia.  Her paternal grandparents were Archadius and Aelia Eudoxia; her maternal grandparents were Leontius and <Unknow
2 CONC n>. She had three sisters named Flaccilla, Marina and Arcadia.
2 CONT --------------------
