1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Al-Khaṭṭāb ibn Nufayl (Arabic: الخطاب بن نفيل) was an Arab chief from the tribe of Quraysh. He lived during the 6th century and was a contemporary of the Islamic prophet Muhammad. His son Umar would later become Mu
2 CONC slim, and would come to be regarded by Sunni Muslims as the second "Rightly guided Caliph" (Arabic "Rashidun"). Thus, he was the ancestor of a good number of the companions of the Prophet (Arabic: ṣaḥāba).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Khattab was the son of Nufayl ibn Abd al-Uzza. His father was the chief of Banu Adi clan of Quraysh. After the death of his father, he succeeded him as the chief of Banu Adi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Khattab had a brother who died young. The name of this bother was Amr ibn Nufayl. Amr had a son named Zayd. He had good relations with his tribesmen and kin. However, his relationship with his nephew Zayd ibn Amr started gradua
2 CONC lly deteriorating because his nephew had denied the subordinate gods to Allāh and he embraced strict monotheism. This angered many members of Nufayl family and Banu Adi clan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Zayd's wife Safiya disliked his travels to Syria. Whenever she saw him preparing for a journey, she reported it to al-Khattab, who would reproach Zayd for abandoning their religion. Zayd did not bother to explain himself to al-Kha
2 CONC ttab, but he rebuked Safiya for trying to humiliate him.[2]: 101, 102  Al-Khattab harassed Zayd so severely that Zayd was forced to leave the city. He spent the last few years of his life in the mountain caves surrounding Mecca. A
2 CONC l-Khattab then instructed the "young irresponsible men of the Quraysh" to ensure that Zayd could never enter the city again. Whenever Zayd tried to enter in secret, al-Khattab's men drove him out again.[2]: 102–103 [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT One time, his nephew Zayd ibn Amr taunted him for worshiping idols.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a son, Umar. His other children included a daughter, Fatimah bint al-Khattab, and a son Zayd ibn al-Khattab. The daughter married the hanif Said ibn Zayd, and later would both become Muslims. However, they hid their new fai
2 CONC th from al-Khattab and Umar. Al-Khattab died between 614 and 616.[4][unreliable source?]
2 CONT
2 CONT In accounts preserved by al-Yahsubi (d. 1149), al-Khattab converted to Islam.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Khattab was the relative of many Companions of Muhammad.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife
2 CONT Hantamah bint Hisham, she belongs to the wealthy clan of Quraysh Banu Makhzum.
2 CONT Asma bint Wahb, was the second wife of al-Khattab
2 CONT Children
2 CONT The children of al-Khattab are:
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar ibn al-Khattab, he was the elder son of Hantamah and Al-Khattab
2 CONT Fatimah bint al-Khattab, daughter of Hantamah and Al-Khattab
2 CONT Zayd ibn al-Khattab, he was the son of Al-Khattab and his second wife Asma bint Wahb.
2 CONT Daughters and sons-in-law
2 CONT Sa'id bin Zayd
2 CONT Zaynab bint Madhun, she married Umar before 605[6]: 56 
2 CONT Umm Kulthum bint Jarwal, she married Umar ibn al-Khattab before 616,[7]: 92 
2 CONT Qurayba bint Abi Umayya, she married Umar before 616.[2]: 510 [7]: 92 
2 CONT Grandchildren
2 CONT Hafsa bint Umar
2 CONT Abdullah ibn Umar
2 CONT Ubaydullah ibn Umar
2 CONT Zayd ibn Umar
2 CONT Asim ibn Umar, famous early Muslim scholar.
2 CONT Abdurrahman ibn Sa'id ibn Zayd, also known as Zayd Abdur Rahman the Elder, was the son of her daughter Fatima.
2 CONT References
2 CONT  Al Maarif, by Ibn Qutaybah page 77, Chapter "Dhikr Umar" [1]
2 CONT  Muhammad ibn Ishaq. Sirat Rasul Allah. Translated by Guillaume, A. (1955). The Life of Muhammad. Oxford: Oxford University Press.
2 CONT  Guillaume, A. (1960). New Light on the Life of Muhammad, p. 27. Manchester: Manchester University Press.
2 CONT  "Archived copy". Archived from the original on 2006-02-26. Retrieved 2006-03-17.
2 CONT  al-Yahsubi, Al-qadi 'Iyad (2013-01-01). Ash - Shifa- Healing through defining The rights of Prophet Muhammad: الشفا بتعريف حقوق المصطفى (ص) [عربي/انكليزي] ترجمة. Dar Al Kotob Al Ilmiyah دار الكتب العلمية. ISBN 978-2-7451-6073-7.
2 CONT  Muhammad ibn Saad. Kitab al-Tabaqat al-Kabir vol. 8. Translated by Bewley, A. (1995). The Women of Madina. London: Ta-Ha Publishers.
2 CONT  Muhammad ibn Jarir al-Tabari. Tarikh al-Rusul wa'l-Muluk. Translated by Fishbein, M. (1997). Volume 8: The Victory of Islam. Albany: State University of New York Press.
