0 @N6000000002187988977@ NOTE Hattusili III
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hattusili III was a king of the Hittite empire (New kingdom) 1265 BC–1235 BC. He was the fourth and last son of Mursili II. Mursili appointed Hattusili as priest of Sausga of Samuha, and Hattusili remained loyal to the "Ishtar of 
1 CONC Samuha" to the end of his days.
1 CONT
1 CONT His older brother Muwatalli II moved his seat in Tarhuntassa and appointed him governor of Hattusa. Hattusili was the commander of Hittite forces in 1274 BC which defeated an Egyptian campign into Syria in the famous Battle of Kad
1 CONC esh. Hattusili as governor also reconquered Nerik and became the High Priest of its storm god. Hattusili named his eldest son and crown prince "Nerikkaili" in honor of this achievement.
1 CONT
1 CONT His nephew, Mursili III (or Urhi-Teshub) moved the capitol back to Hattusa (KBo 21.15 i 11-12), rendering Hattusili's governorship redundant. Mursili then deposed him from Nerik, triggering a civil war. Hattusili defeated and exil
1 CONC ed his nephew. Hattusili renamed Urhi-Teshup's sibling Ulmi-Teshup "Kurunta" (or: married her off to Kurunta), and appointed Kurunta over Tarhuntassa in his place. After this, Hattusili elevated a junior son Tudhaliya IV as crown 
1 CONC prince instead.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hattusili and the Egyptian pharaoh Ramesses II signed the first written peace treaty in history, establishing a long-lasting peace between the two rival empires. Ramesses married Hattusili's daughter Maathorneferure.
1 CONT
1 CONT An archive of over 200 letters have been found from the royal palace at Hattusa which show that Hattusili exchanged with letters with numerous Near Eastern kings including Ramesses II of ancient Egypt. They are an important primar
1 CONC y source for this period.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Mursili III Hittite king Succeeded by:
1 CONT Tudhaliya IV
0 @N6000000002187608275@ NOTE Maathorneferure
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Maathorneferure was a princess of Hittite empire, and was married to the Egyptian Pharaoh Ramesses II in the 34th year of his reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was the daughter of Hattusili III and his wife Puduchepa. Her real name is unknown, but her Egyptian name translates as "One who sees Horus, the invisible splendor of Ra".
1 CONT
1 CONT At Tanis, there is a broken statue of Ramesses that shows her (mostly destroyed) figure touching his leg, together with her cartouche.
0 @N6000000002187991051@ NOTE Mursili II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mursili II was a king of the Hittite Empire (New kingdom) from ca. 1322 BC to ca. 1295 BC. He was the younger son of Suppiluliuma I. RH Beal believes that before his accession, his name was written in Luwian hieroglyphic seals wit
1 CONC h the combined Sumerian and Akkadian glyphs, GAL-MEŠEDI (NABU 2001/4); which elsewhere is understood as the title, "chief of the bodyguards".
1 CONT
1 CONT This prince assumed the throne after the premature death of his elder brother Arnuwanda II, like their father victim to the plague ravaging the empire in the 1320s; and likely took the name "Mursili" afterward. He faced numerous r
1 CONC ebellions early in his reign, the most serious of which were by the Kaskas in the mountains of Anatolia. The annals for the first ten years of his reign have survived, and they reveal that an "omen of the sun," or solar eclipse, o
1 CONC ccurred in his tenth year as king, just as he was about to launch his campaign against the Kaska peoples. While Mursili II's highest confirmed year date was his twenty-second, it is believed that he enjoyed a reign of around 25-30
1 CONC  years. He was succeeded by Muwatalli.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 The eclipse
1 CONT     * 2 Mursili II in fiction
1 CONT     * 3 References
1 CONT     * 4 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT The eclipse
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Mursili's eclipse
1 CONT
1 CONT Mursili's eclipse is of great importance for the absolute dating of the Hittite Empire within the chronology of the Ancient Near East. There are only two possible dates for the eclipse: 13 April 1308 BC or 24 June 1312 BC. The 131
1 CONC 2 date is accepted by most Hittitologists, e.g. Trevor Bryce (1998), while Paul Astrom (1993) suggested the 1308 date.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Mursili II in fiction
1 CONT
1 CONT A fictionalized version of Mursili II appears in the Japanese manga Red River. He is "Kail Mursili", which according to Beal would be an anachronism - since he is not named "Mursili" in any texts prior to those of his own reign, a
1 CONC nd was likely named something else. Kail is one of the comic's main protagonists and is portrayed as noble and brave, as well as a playboy.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Paul Astrom, 'The Omen of the Sun in the Tenth Year of the Reign of Mursilis II', in Horizons and Styles: Studies in Early Art and Archaeology in Honour of Professor Homer L. Thomas (1993)
1 CONT     * Trevor Bryce, The Kingdom of the Hittites (1998)
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Arnuwanda II Hittite king Succeeded by:
1 CONT Muwatalli
0 @N6000000002187991065@ NOTE Muwatalli II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Muwatalli II was a king of the New kingdom of the Hittite empire (1295–1272 BC). The eldest surviving son of Mursili II, he is best known as the Hittite ruler who fought Ramesses II to a standstill at the Battle of Kadesh around 1
1 CONC 274 BC. After becoming king, he relocated the capitol to Tarhuntassa when the Kaskas invaded; appointing his brother Hattusili as governor in Hattusa.
1 CONT
1 CONT A copy of a treaty between him and Alaksandu, ruler of Wilusa, one of the Arzawa lands has been recovered. Egyptologists also believe that Muwattalli reached an informal peace treaty or understanding with Seti I over Kadesh to avo
1 CONC id a clash between the two superpowers over control of Syria. In it, Seti I effectively ceded Kadesh to the Hittite king in order to focus on domestic issues in Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT Muwatalli had at least two children, both given Hurrian names in honor of the Hurrian storm god Teshup. One was a son, Urhi-Teshup, who became king Mursili III until Hattusili III deposed him. Another was possibly a daughter, Ulmi
1 CONC -Teshup ("female-slave of Teshup"); whom Hattusili III either married to king Kurunta in Tarhuntassa, or else (if male) was renamed Kurunta and appointed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Muwatalli's namesake, Muwatalli I, was a pre-Empire king of the early 14th century, the predecessor of Tudhaliya I.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Reign of Muwatalli II
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Mursili II Hittite king Succeeded by:
1 CONT Mursili III
0 @N6000000002187608231@ NOTE Ramesses II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Usermaatre-setepenre
1 CONT The Justice of Re is Powerful,
1 CONT Chosen of Re
1 CONT Golden Horus Userrenput-aanehktu
1 CONT Nebty name Mekkemetwafkhasut
1 CONT Horus name Kanakht Merymaa
1 CONT Reign 66 years
1 CONT 1279 BC to 1213 BC
1 CONT Consort(s) Isetnofret Nefertari
1 CONT Maathorneferure
1 CONT Issues Bintanath Sethnakht
1 CONT Khaemweset Merneptah
1 CONT Amun-her-khepsef Meritamen
1 CONT Father Seti I
1 CONT Mother Queen Tuya
1 CONT Born 1302 BC
1 CONT Died 1213 BC
1 CONT Burial KV7
1 CONT Major
1 CONT Monuments Abu Simbel, Ramesseum,
1 CONT etc.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramesses II (also known as Ramesses the Great and alternatively transcribed as Ramses and Rameses) was an Egyptian pharaoh of the nineteenth dynasty. He was born ca. 1302 BC. At age fourteen, Ramses II was appointed Prince Regent 
1 CONC by his father. He is believed to have taken the throne in his early 20s and to have ruled Egypt from 1279 BC to 1213 BC[1] for a total of 66 years and 2 months. He was once said to have lived to be 99 years old, but it is more lik
1 CONC ely that he died in his 90th or 92nd year. Ancient Greek writers such as Herodotus attributed his accomplishments to the semi-mythical Sesostris, and he is traditionally believed to have been the Pharaoh of the Exodus. If he becam
1 CONC e king in 1279 BC as most Egyptologists today believe, he would have taken the throne on May 31, 1279 BC based on his known accession date of III Shemu day 27[2].
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Naming
1 CONT     * 2 Life
1 CONT     * 3 Mummy
1 CONT     * 4 Was he the Pharaoh of Exodus?
1 CONT     * 5 Fiction
1 CONT     * 6 See also
1 CONT     * 7 References
1 CONT     * 8 Further reading
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Naming
1 CONT
1 CONT As with most pharaohs, Ramesses had a number of names. The two most important, his praenomen (regnal name) and nomen (birth name) are shown in Egyptian hieroglyphs above to the right. These names are transliterated as wsr-m3‘t-r‘–
1 CONC stp-n-r‘ r‘-ms-sw–mry-?-mn, which is usually written as Usermaatra-setepenra Ramessu-meryamen. It translates as "Powerful one of Maat, the Justice of Ra is Powerful, chosen of Ra, Ra bore him, beloved of Amun". In the Hittite copy
1 CONC  of the above-mentioned peace treaty with Hattusilis, the Pharaoh's name appears as Washmuaria Shatepnaria Riamashesha Maiamana. Some scholars believe this is possibly a closer approximation of the actual vocalization of the Egypt
1 CONC ian king's name.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Life
1 CONT Tablet of the treaty between Hattusili III of Hatti and Ramesses II of Egypt, contained within the Istanbul Archaeology Museum.
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT Tablet of the treaty between Hattusili III of Hatti and Ramesses II of Egypt, contained within the Istanbul Archaeology Museum.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramesses II was the third king of the 19th dynasty, and the second son of Seti I and his Queen Tuya. Ramessses' older brother predeceased him before adulthood. The most memorable of Ramesses' wives was Nefertari. Earlier wives, am
1 CONC ong others, were Isisnofret and Maathorneferure[3], Princess of Hatti. The writer Terence Gray stated in 1923 that Ramesses II had as many as 20 sons and 20 daughters; more recent scholars, however, believe his offspring were far 
1 CONC fewer. His children include Bintanath and Meritamen (princesses and their father's wives), Sethnakhte, the Pharaoh Merneptah (who succeeded him), and prince Khaemweset.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramesses led several expeditions north into the lands east of the Mediterranean (the location of the modern Israel, Lebanon and Syria). At the Second Battle of Kadesh towards the end of the fourth year of his reign (1274 BC), Egyp
1 CONC tian forces under his leadership engaged the forces of Muwatallis, king of the Hittites. The battle almost turned to disaster as Ramesses fell into a well-laid trap set by Muwatallis whose infantry and chariotry were well hidden b
1 CONC ehind the eastern bank of the Orontes river under the command of the king's brother, Hattusili III. The Egyptian army had been divided into two main forces – the Re and Amun brigades with Ramesses and the Ptah and Seth brigades – 
1 CONC separated from each other by forests and the far side of the Orontes river[4]. The Re brigade was almost totally destroyed by the surprise Hittite chariot attack and Ramesses II had barely enough time to rally his own Amun brigade
1 CONC  and secure enough reinforcements from the Ptah Army Brigade (who were just arriving upon the scene) to turn the tide against the Hittites. While Ramesses II in theory 'won' the battle, Muwatallis effectively won the war because t
1 CONC he Pharaoh was compelled to retreat south while the Hittite commander Hattusili III relentlessy harried the surviving Egyptian forces as far as Damascus, according to the Hittite records at Boghazkoy. [5]
1 CONT
1 CONT Egypt's sphere of influence was now restricted to Canaan while Syria fell into Hittite hands. Over the ensuing years, Rameses II would return to campaign against the Hittites and even achieved several spectacular victories (at a t
1 CONC ime of Hittite weakness due to a dispute over Muwatallis' succession) to briefly capture the cities of Tunip, where no Egyptian soldier had been seen since the time of Thutmose III and the Kadesh in his 8th Year. However, neither 
1 CONC power could effectively defeat the other in battle. Consequently, in the twenty-first year of his reign (1258 BC), Ramses decided to conclude an agreement with the new Hittite king at Kadesh, Hattusili III, to end the conflict. Th
1 CONC e ensuing document is the earliest known peace treaty in world history.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramesses II also campaigned south of the first cataract into Nubia. He constructed many impressive monuments, including the renowned archeological complex of Abu Simbel, and the mortuary temple known as the Ramesseum. It is said t
1 CONC hat there are more statues of him in existence than of any other Egyptian pharaoh [6], not surprising as he was the second-longest-reigning Pharaoh by many years (second to Pepi II).
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT He was buried in the Valley of the Kings, in KV7, but his mummy was later moved to the mummy cache at Deir el-Bahri, where it was found in 1881 and placed in the Egyptian Museum in Cairo five years later, where it is still exhibit
1 CONC ed with pride by the Egyptian people. Ramesses' mummy featured a hooked nose, strong jaw and was of above average height for an ancient Egyptian, standing some five feet and seven inches tall.[7] He suffered from arthritis in his 
1 CONC joints, tooth cavities and poor circulation during the last years of his life. [8]. His successor was ultimately to be his thirteenth son Merneptah.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Was he the Pharaoh of Exodus?
1 CONT
1 CONT At least as early as Eusebius of Caesarea, Ramesses II was identified with the pharaoh of whom the Biblical figure Moses demanded his people be released from slavery.
1 CONT
1 CONT The identification is sometimes disputed for several reasons:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ramesses II was not drowned in the Sea, although the biblical account makes no specific claim that the pharaoh was with his army when they were "swept ... into the sea." In fact, Jewish tradition says that he was the only Eg
1 CONC yptian first born to have survived and would later become the King of Nineveh in the Book of Jonah. [9]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * There is nothing in the archaeological record from the time of his reign to confirm the Plagues of Egypt, which is somewhat surprising. In addition, there is nothing in the royal Egyptian textual records. Although there are 
1 CONC a large number of Egyptian texts still in existence, this isn't especially weird, given the style of history-writing of the time. No pharaoh would write down a major defeat, even catastrophic one (as it is described in Biblical na
1 CONC rrative) the same manner his victories were documented.
1 CONT
1 CONT     * The dates now ascribed to Ramesses's reign by most modern scholars do not match the dates when Moses was believed to be in Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT Scholars like George Mendenhall[10] associate the Israelite's arrival in Palestine more closely with the Hapiru mentioned in the Amarna letters and Hittite treaties of that time than with Ramesses II.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Bible does however state that the Israelites toiled in slavery and build "for Pharaoh supply cities, Pithom and Ra'amses" in the Egyptian Delta. [11] The latter city is probably a reference to the city of Pi-Ramesse Aa-nakhtu 
1 CONC or the "House of Ramesses, Great-of-Victories" (modern day Qantir) which had been Seti I's summer retreat[12]. Ramesses II greatly enlarged this city as his principal northern capital and as an important forward base for his milit
1 CONC ary campaigns into the Levant and his control over Canaan. According to Kenneth Kitchen, Pi-Ramesses was largely abandoned from c.1130 BC onwards; as was often the practice, later rulers removed much of the stone from the city to 
1 CONC build the temples of their new capital: Tanis. [13]. Therefore if the identification of the city is correct, it strengthens the case for identifying Ramesses II as the Pharaoh who reign during Moses' lifetime.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the other hand, Ramses' own stele erected in the late 13th century BC in the city known to the Bible as Bet-Shan mentions two conquered peoples who came to "make obeisance to him" in his city of Ramses but mentions neither the 
1 CONC building of the city nor, as some have written, the Israelites or Hapiru [14].
1 CONT
1 CONT Speculation that Ramesses II was the Biblical Pharaoh named Shishak who attacked Judah and seized war bounty from Jerusalem in Year 5 of Rehoboam is impossible because both Ramesses II and his 19th Dynasty successors (ie: Mernepta
1 CONC h, Seti II, Siptah, Twosret) retained firm control over Canaan during their reigns. Neither Israel nor Judah could have existed as independent states at this time.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Fiction
1 CONT
1 CONT The life of Ramesses II has also inspired a large number of historical novels, including the five volume series, Ramsès, by the French writer Christian Jacq. (Translated editions are available for non-French readers.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramesses is also claimed as a King of Quendor in Zork I although he is not mentioned in any of the games or literature in the series.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramesses was the main character in the Anne Rice book The Mummy or Ramses the Damned.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramesses was portrayed by Yul Brynner in the classic film The Ten Commandments (1956).
1 CONT
1 CONT In the film "The Prince of Egypt" Ramesses is portrayed as Moses' adoptive brother.
1 CONT
1 CONT The song "User-Maat-Re" by death metal band Nile is about Ramesses II.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT See also
1 CONT
