1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Burial
1 CONT Seti's magnificient tomb (KV17) was found in 1817 by Giovanni Battista Belzoni, in the Valley of the Kings, but his Mummy was not discovered until 1881, in tomb DB320 at Deir el-Bahri, and has since been kept at Giza Museum. (His 
1 CONC sarcophagus is in the Sir John Soane's Museum, in London, England). From an examination of his extremely well preserved mummy, Seti I appears to have been less than forty years old when he died unexpectedly. This is in stark contr
1 CONC ast to the situation with Horemheb, Rameses I and Rameses II who all lived to an advanced age. The reasons for his relatively early death are uncertain, but there is no evidence of violence on the mummy to suggest that he was murd
1 CONC ered. It has been suggested that he died from a disease which had affected him for years, possibly related to his heart. The latter was found placed in the right part of the body, while the usual practice of the day was to place i
1 CONC t in the left part during the mummification process. Opinions vary whether this was a mistake, an attempt to have Seti's heart work better in his afterlife than it did during his lifetime or even that Seti was born with his heart 
1 CONC on the right side of his body, a rare occurrence.
