0 @N6000000002187608392@ NOTE Queen Sitre
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Queen Sitre ("Daughter of Re") was the Great Royal Wife of Pharaoh Ramesses I of Egypt and mother of Seti I.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was buried in a tomb in the Valley of the Queens (QV38). The decoration was unfinished, consisting of just line drawings.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Grajetkzi, Wolfram (2005) Ancient Egyptian Queens – a hieroglyphic dictionary
0 @N6000000002187991107@ NOTE Tudhaliya II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Tudhaliya II (also Tudhaliya III) was a king of the Hittite empire (New kingdom) 1430 BC–1400 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Hittite empire suffered serious loss of territory during Tudhaliya's reign, with even the capital itself being burnt down. But, under the able guidance of Tudhaliya's son, the future Suppiluliuma I, the Hittites began to make 
1 CONC a recovery while Tudhaliya still sat on the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Proper numbering of the Hittite rulers who bore the name Tudhaliya is problematical. There was a Hattian era figure who bore the name Tudhaliya who may or may not have ruled as king. Other reconstructions insert a Tudhaliya direct
1 CONC ly after Muwattalli I, but before the more famous ruler frequently credited with founding the Empire. The Tudhaliya discussed in this article will be generally found as either "the second" or "the third".
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Arnuwanda I Hittite king Succeeded by:
1 CONT Hattusili II (?)
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Reign of Tudhaliya II (Tudhaliya III at this link)
0 @N6000000002187991116@ NOTE Tudhaliya III
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Tudhaliya III was a short-lived king of the Hittite Empire (New Kingdom); He may have been the son and successor of Hattusili II, however he is normally viewed as the son of Tudhaliya II (according to the king count used here). He
1 CONC  is never explicitly known to have been king at all. The texts refer to him as "Tudhaliya the child" (or, as an alternate translation, "Tudhaliya the Young(er)". He was killed by a group of officers that included his successor, an
1 CONC d quite probably brother, Suppiluliuma I.
1 CONT
1 CONT There is some difficulty concerning numbering of the Tudhaliyas (and Hattusilis) of the Hittite empire. This Tudhaliya is normally not included in Hittite king lists, and his father will often be seen listed as Tudhaliya III.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Hattusili II(?) Hittite king Succeeded by:
1 CONT Suppiluliuma I
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Reign of Suppiluliuma I, discussing Tudhaliya
0 @N6000000002187608109@ NOTE Ramesses VI
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramesses VI (also written Ramses and Rameses) (reigned 1142 BC to 1134 BC) was the fifth ruler of the Twentieth dynasty of Egypt and his tomb, KV9, is located near King Tutankhamen's in the Valley of the Kings.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramesses' two main names are normally realised as Nebmaatre-meryamun and Ramesse-amunherkhepshef neterhekaiunu. Based on Raphael Ventura's successful reconstruction of Turin Papyrus 1907+1908, Ramesses VI is now known to have enjo
1 CONC yed a reign of 8 Full Years. He lived for 2 months into his brief Regnal Year 9 before dying and was succeeded by his son, Ramesses VII. His 8th Regnal Year is also attested in a graffito which names the then serving High Priest o
1 CONC f Amun, Ramessessnakht.
1 CONT
1 CONT Shortly after his burial, his tomb was penetrated and ransacked by grave robbers who hacked away at his hands and feet in order to gain access to his jewellry. A medical examination of his Mummy reveals severe damage to his body.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ramesses V Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT Twentieth Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ramesses VII
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Raphael Ventura, "More Chronological Evidence from Turin Papyrus Cat.1907+1908," JNES 42, No.4 (1983), pp.271-277
0 @N6000000002187608027@ NOTE Ramesses IV
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Heqamaatre Ramesses IV (also written Ramses or Rameses) was the third pharaoh of the Twentieth Dynasty of the New Kingdom of Ancient Egypt. He was the fifth son of Ramesses III but assumed the throne since all four of his older br
1 CONC others predeceased him. Due to the three decade rule of his father, he is believed to have been in his 40s when he took the throne. His reign has been dated to either 1152 to 1146 BC or 1154 to 1148 BC.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Projects
1 CONT
1 CONT He attempted a building campaign on the scale of Ramesses II, doubling the size of the work gangs at Deir el-Medina to a total of 120 men, and dispatched several large expeditions to the stone quarries of Wadi Hammamat and the Sin
1 CONC ai. However, he did not live long enough to accomplish his goals. Part of his program included the extensive enlargement of his father's Temple of Khonsu at Karnak and the construction of a large mortuary temple near the Temple of
1 CONC  Hatshepsut.
1 CONT
1 CONT The most important document to survive from this pharaoh's rule is the Papyrus Harris I, which honours the life of his father, Ramesses III, by listing the latter's many accomplishments and gifts to the temples of Egypt, and the T
1 CONC urin papyrus, the earliest known geologic map.
1 CONT
1 CONT After a short 6 Year reign, Ramesses IV died and was buried in tomb KV2 in the Valley of the Kings. His chief wife was Queen Tentopet who was buried in QV74. His prenomen or throne name, Heqamaatre, means "Ruler of Justice like Re
1 CONC ."
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ramesses IV: Beginning of the Empire's collapse
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ramesses III Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT Twentieth Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ramesses V
0 @N6000000002187705416@ NOTE Ramesses V
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramesses V (also written Ramses and Rameses) (reigned 1146 BC to 1142 BC) was the fourth pharaoh of the Twentieth dynasty of Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the brother of Ramesses IV. His reign was characterized by the continued growth of the power of the priesthood of Amon, which controlled much of the temple land in the country and was in control of the financial system thro
1 CONC ugh the high priest's son. The papyrus Turin 1887 records a financial scandal during his reign that involved the priests of Elephantine.
1 CONT
1 CONT The circumstances of his death are unknown. It is possible he was dethroned by his successor, Ramesses VI. His mummy has been recovered and seems to indicate that he died of smallpox, but there is a major head wound evident as wel
1 CONC l.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ramesses IV Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT Twentieth Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ramesses VI 
0 @N6000000002187608128@ NOTE Ramesses VIII
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Usermaatre Akhenamun Ramesses VIII (also written Ramses and Rameses) Sethherkhepshef Meryamun (1126-1124 BC) was the seventh Pharaoh of the Twentieth Dynasty of the New Kingdom of Ancient Egypt and is thought to have been a son of
1 CONC  Ramesses III. He is the most obscure ruler of this Dynasty and the current information from his brief kingship suggest that he lasted on the throne for one or two years at most. Monuments from his reign are almost non-existent an
1 CONC d consist primarily of an inscription at Medinet Habu, a mention of this ruler in one stela--Berlin stela 2081 of Hori at Abydos--and one scarab. His only known date is a Year 1, I Peret day 2 graffito in the tomb of Kyenebu at Th
1 CONC ebes [Tomb No.113: see P.M. I, i (1960), pp. 230-231]
1 CONT
1 CONT Of all the Pharaohs of the Twentieth Dynasty, his tomb is the only one which has not been identified in the Valley of the Kings, though some scholars have suggested that the tomb of Prince Mentuherkhepshef, KV19, the son of Ramess
1 CONC es IX, was originally started for Ramesses VIII but proved unsuitable when he became a king in his own right.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ramesses VII Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT Twentieth Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ramesses IX
0 @N6000000002187705653@ NOTE Ashur-uballit I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashur-uballit I, or Assur-uballit I, was king of the Assyrian empire (c. 1365 BC to 1330 BC). His reign marks Assyria's independence from the kingdom of Mitanni, by defeating Shuttarna II; and the beginning of Assyria's emergence 
1 CONC as a powerful empire. Later on, due to disorder in Babylonia following the death of Burnaburiash II, Ashur-uballit established his grandson Kurigalzu II on the Babylonian throne, in the first of what would become a series of Assyr
1 CONC ian interventions in Babylonian affairs.
1 CONT
1 CONT From the Amarna letters, a series of diplomatic letters from various Middle Eastern monarchs to Amenhotep III and Akhenaten of Egypt, we find two letters from Ashur-uballit I, the second being a follow-up letter to the first. In t
1 CONC he letters, Ashur-uballit refers to his second predecessor Ashur-nadin-ahhe II as his "father" or "ancestor," rather than his actual father, Eriba-Adad I, which has led some critics of conventional Egyptian chronology, such as Dav
1 CONC id Rohl, to claim that the Ashur-uballit of the Amarna letters was not the same as Ashur-uballit I. This, however, ignores the fact that monarchs in the Amarna letters frequently refer to predecessors as their "father," even if th
1 CONC ey were not their biological sons. In this case, Ashur-uballit presumably referred to Ashur-nadin-ahhe because the latter, unlike Eriba-Adad I, had previously corresponded with the Egyptian court.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Eriba-Adad I King of Assyria
1 CONT 1365–1330 BCE Succeeded by:
1 CONT Enlil-nirari 
