0 @N6000000002187610695@ NOTE Ramesses VII
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Nebmaatre Meryamun Ramesses VII (also written Ramses and Rameses) (reigned from 1134 BC to 1126 BC). He was the sixth pharaoh of the 20th dynasty of Ancient Egypt and the son of Ramesses VI. The Turin Accounting Papyrus 1907+1908 
1 CONC dates to Year 7 of his reign and states that 11 Full Years passed from Year 5 of his father to Year 7 of his reign[1].
1 CONT
1 CONT He was buried in Tomb KV1 upon his death after a reign of 8 years. His mummy has never been found.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ramesses VI Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT Twentieth Dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ramesses VIII
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Raphael Ventura. JNES 42, Vol.4(1983), pp.271-277
0 @N6000000002187610762@ NOTE Ramesses IX
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramesses IX (also written Ramses and Rameses) (1124 BC – 1106 BC) was the eighth king of the Twentieth dynasty of Egypt. He was the second longest ruling king of this Dynasty at 18 Years.
1 CONT
1 CONT His reign is best known for a scandal from his 16th to 19th Regnal Years, when the royal tombs in the Theban necropolis were robbed. An inspection, led by Paser, mayor of eastern Thebes, pointed to Paweraa, mayor of western Thebes
1 CONC , as being the culprit behind this wave of robberies. However, it proved impossible for Paweraa to be officially charged with the crimes, and according to Egyptologist Joyce Tyldesley, Paser disappeared from sight soon after he fi
1 CONC led his report.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramesses IX's son, Montuherkhopshef, did not live to succeed his father, though Montuherkhopshef has one of the most beautiful tombs in the Valley of the Kings (designated KV19). The throne was assumed by Ramesses X whose relation
1 CONC ship to Ramesses IX is still unclear. The tomb of Ramesses IX is in the Valley of the Kings (KV6) and has been open since antiquity, as evidenced by Roman and Greek graffiti on the tomb walls. In 1881, the mummy of Ramesses IX was
1 CONC  found in the Deir el-Bahri cache (DB320).
1 CONT
1 CONT   This ancient Egyptian article is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
