0 @N6000000002187610797@ NOTE Amenemopet
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT King Usimare Amenemopet (or Amenemope) was the son of Psusennes I and Queen Mutnedjemet. He served as a junior co-regent in his father's last years according to the evidence from a mummy bandage. Both Psusennes I and Amenemopet's 
1 CONC royal tombs were discovered intact by Pierre Montet in the 1939-40 Egypt excavation season and were filled with vast treasures including gold funerary masks, coffins and numerous other items of precious jewelry. Montet opened Amen
1 CONC emopet's tomb in April 1940 – just a month before the German invasion of France and the Low Countries in World War Two. Thereafter, all excavation work abruptly ceased until the end of the war. Montet resumed his excavation work a
1 CONC t Tanis in 1946 and later published his findings in 1958.
1 CONT
1 CONT Four objects from Amenemopet's tomb preserve the name of his illustrious father, Psusennes I including a collar and several bracelets. Manetho states in his Epitome that Amenemopet enjoyed a reign of nine years. His funerary mask 
1 CONC renders a youthful depiction of the king.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External references
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Edward Wente, "On the Chronology of the Twenty-First Dynasty," JNES 26(1967), p.156
1 CONT     * The Funerary Mask of Amenemope
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Psusennes I Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT 992 – 983 BC
1 CONT Twenty-first dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Osorkon the Elder
