0 @N6000000002187705548@ NOTE Ninurta-apal-Ekur
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ninurta-apal-Ekur was a King of Assyria. There is some dispute as to how long he reigned, based on disputes among various copies of the Assyrian King List. The Nassouhi King List, sometimes considered to be older than the other ve
1 CONC rsions of the King List we have, gives him 13 years of reign, but the other king lists give him only 3. More recent scholarship has tended to support the shorter reign, in which case he reigned from 1182 to 1179 BC (alternately, h
1 CONC e reigned from 1193 to 1180 BC). He was a usurper, the great-great-great grandson of Adad-nirari I. He seized the throne from Enlil-kudurri-usur, a son of the powerful king Tukulti-Ninurta I. After his reign of either three or thi
1 CONC rteen years, Ninurta-apal-Ekur was succeeded by his son, Ashur-dan I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Enlil-kudurri-usur King of Assyria
1 CONT 1182–1179 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ashur-dan I 
0 @N6000000002187604596@ NOTE Osorkon I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of Shoshenq I and Queen Karomama, Sekhemkheperre Osorkon I was the second king of Egypt's 22nd Dynasty and ruled around 922 BC-887 BC. He succeeded his father Shoshenq I who died within 2-3 years of his successful 925 BC B
1 CONC iblical campaign against Ancient Israel and Judah. Osorkon I's reign was known for many temple building projects and was a long and prosperous period of Egypt's History. His Highest known date is a "Year 33 Second Heb Sed" inscrip
1 CONC tion found on the bandage of Nakhtefmut's Mummy which held a bracellet inscribed with Osorkon I's prenomen: Sekhemkheperre. This date can only belong to Osorkon I since no other early Dynasty 22 king ruled for close to 30 Years un
1 CONC til the time of Osorkon II. Other Mummy linens which belong to his reign include 3 separate bandages dating to his Year 11, 12 and 23 on the Mummy of Khonsmaakheru in Berlin. The bandages are dated anonymous but definitely belong 
1 CONC to his reign because Khonsmaakheru wore leather braces that contained a menat-tab naming Osorkon I. (see Altenmüller below) Secondly, no other king who ruled around Osorkon I's reign had a 23rd Regnal Year including Shoshenq I who
1 CONC  died in his Year 22. While Manetho gives Osorkon I a reign of 15 Years in his Epitome, this is most likely an error for 35 Years based on the evidence of the Second Heb Sed bandage, as Kenneth Kitchen notes.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Osorkon I's successor
1 CONT     * 2 Aftermath
1 CONT     * 3 Bibliography
1 CONT     * 4 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Osorkon I's successor
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Osorkon I is thought to have been directly succeeded by his son Takelot I, it is possible that another ruler, Heqakheperre Shoshenq II, intervened briefly between these two kings because Takelot I was a son of Osorkon I t
1 CONC hrough Queen Tashedkhons-a minor secondary wife of this king. In contrast, Osorkon I's senior wife was Queen Maatkare, who may have been Shoshenq II's mother. However, Shoshenq II could also have been another son of Shoshenq I sin
1 CONC ce the latter was the only other king to be mentioned in objects from Shoshenq II's intact royal tomb at Tanis aside from Shoshenq II himself. These objects are inscribed with either Shoshenq I's prenomen--Hedjkheperre Shoshenq--o
1 CONC r Shoshenq, Chief of the Ma--which was Shoshenq I's title before he became king. Since Derry's forensic examination of his Mummy reveals him to be a Man in his 50's upon his death, Shoshenq II could have lived beyond Osorkon I's 3
1 CONC 5 Year reign and assumed the throne for a few short years.
1 CONT
1 CONT While Kenneth A. Kitchen, in his invaluable Third Intermediate Period of Egypt book, views Shoshenq II as the High Priest of Amun at Thebes Shoshenq C, and a short-lived coregent of Osorkon I who predeceased his father, the equall
1 CONC y well respected German Egyptologist J. Von Beckerath, in his 1997 book Chronologie des Pharaonischen Ägypten, maintains that Shoshenq II was more far more likely to be an independent king of Tanis who ruled the 22nd Dynasty in hi
1 CONC s own right for c.2 Years (see page 94-98 for Beckerath's treatment of the 22nd Dynasty). This hypothesis is supported by the fact that Shoshenq II employed a complete royal titulary along with a distinct prenomen Heqakheperre and
1 CONC  his intact tomb at Tanis was filled with numerous treasures including jewelled pectorals and bracellets, an impressive falconheaded silver coffin and a gold face mask–items which indicate a genuine king of the 22nd Dynasty. More 
1 CONC significantly, however, no mention of Osorkon I's name was preserved on any ushabtis, jars, jewelry or other objects within Shoshenq II's tomb. This situation would be improbable if he was indeed Osorkon I's son, and was buried by
1 CONC  his father, as Kitchen's Chronology suggests. These facts, taken together, imply that Sheshonq II ruled on his own accord at Tanis and was not a mere coregent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Manetho's Epitome states that "3 Kings" separate Osorkon I from Takelot I. (Beckerath, Chronologie, p.95) This could be an error on Manetho's part or an allusion to Shoshenq II's intervening reign. It may also be a reference to th
1 CONC e recently discovered early Dynasty 22 king Tutkheperre Shoshenq, whose existence is now corroborated by an architectural block from the Great Temple of Bubastis, where Osorkon I and Osorkon II are well attested monumentally. (Eva
1 CONC  Lange, GM 203)
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Aftermath
1 CONT
1 CONT Osorkon I's reign in Egypt was peaceful and uneventful; however, both his son and grandson, Takelot I and Osorkon II respectively, later encountered difficulties controlling Thebes and Upper Egypt in their own reigns since they ha
1 CONC d to deal with a rival king: Harsiese A. Osorkon I's tomb has never been found.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Bibliography
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Hartwig Altenmüller, "Lederbänder und Lederanhänger von der Mumie des Chonsu-maacheru" and "Die Mumienbinden des Chonsu-maacheru " in Alt-Ägypten 30(2000), pp.73-76, 88-89, 102-114. [1]
1 CONT     * J. Von Beckerath, Chronologie des Pharaonischen Ägypten or 'Chronology of the Egyptian Pharaohs,'(Mainz: 1997), Philip Zon Zabern
1 CONT     * Kenneth Kitchen, The Third Intermediate Period in Egypt (1100–650 BC) 3rd ed, (Warminster: 1996), Aris & Phillips Limited
1 CONT     * Eva Lange, "Ein Neuer König Schoschenk in Bubastis," GM 203(2004), pp.65-71
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * List of Pharaohs
1 CONT     * Osorkon Dynasty at ancientworlds.net
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Shoshenq I Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT 922 – 887 BC
1 CONT Twenty-second dynasty of Egypt Succeeded by:
1 CONT Shoshenq II
0 @N6000000002187604577@ NOTE Takelot I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hedjkheperre Setepenre Takelot I was a son of Osorkon I and Queen Tashedkhons, and the father of Osorkon II. He ruled Egypt for 13 Years according to Manetho. Initially, he was believed to be an ephemeral Dynasty 22 Pharaoh since 
1 CONC no monuments at Tanis or Lower Egypt could be conclusively linked to his reign, or mentioned his existence, except for the famous Pasenhor Serapeum stela which dates to Year 37 of Shoshenq V. However, since the late 1980's, Egypto
1 CONC logists have assigned several documents mentioning a king Takelot in Lower Egypt to him rather than Takelot II. Takelot I's reign was relatively short when compared to the three decades-long reigns of his father Osorkon I and son,
1 CONC  Osorkon II. Takelot I, rather than Takelot II, was the king Hedjkheperre Setepenre Takelot who is attested by a Year 9 stela from Bubastis as well as the owner of a partly robbed Royal Tomb at Tanis which belonged to this ruler. 
1 CONC Evidently, both king Takelots used the same prenomen or royal name: Hedjkheperre Setepenre. The main difference between Takelot I and II is that Takelot I never employed the Theban inspired epithet 'Si-Ese' (Son of Isis) in his ti
1 CONC tulary, unlike Takelot II.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Tomb
1 CONT
1 CONT Evidence that the Royal Tanite tomb belonged to Takelot I was suggested long ago by the presence of grave goods found within the burial which mentioned his known parents: namely a Gold Bracelet and Alabaster Jar of Osorkon I, and 
1 CONC a Ushabti figure of Queen Tashedkhons. In addition, a heart scarab found in the king's burial gave his name simply as "Takelot Meryamun" without the Si-Ese epithet used by Takelot II. Recent confirmation of this circumstantial evi
1 CONC dence was published by Karl Jansen-Winkeln in 1987 in Varia Aegyptiaca(VA), whose examination of several inscriptions written on the tomb's walls proved beyond doubt that the person buried here could only be Takelot I, Osorkon II'
1 CONC s father.
1 CONT
1 CONT Osorkon II arranged for this aforementioned inscription to be carved on the Tomb Walls:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "[Made?] by the King of the South & North Egypt, Lord of Both Lands, Usimare Setepenamun, Son of Re, Lord of Crowns, Osorkon II Meryamun, [to furbish] the Osiris (ie: deceased) King Takelot Meryamun in his Mansion which is [an
1 CONC  abode] of the Sun-disc: I have caused him to rest in this Mansion in the vicinity of 'Hidden-of Name' (Amun), according to the doing by a son of benefactions for his father, [to] furbish the one who has made his fortune in confor
1 CONC mity with that Horus Son-of-Isis, commanded for his father, Wennufer." Above the inscription was carved the cartouche of Osorkon II and the text: "A Son, furbishing the one who created (ie: begot) him." (English translation of Jan
1 CONC sen-Winkeln's paper by KA Kitchen, TIPE 1996, xxii-xxiii)
1 CONT
1 CONT This Text shows that Osorkon II honoured his father by burying or reburying him in the Tanite royal tomb complex. Takelot I's final resting place, forms the third chamber of Osorkon II's tomb: ie: Osorkon II interred his father wi
1 CONC thin the walls of his own tomb. Takelot I was buried in an usurped Middle Kingdom sarcophagus that was inscribed with his own cartouche.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Authority
1 CONT
1 CONT Takelot I's authority was not fully recognised in Upper Egypt, and Harsiese A(=Horus, Son of Isis A), or another local Theban king, challenged his power there. Several Nile Quay Texts at Thebes mention two sons of Osorkon I namely
1 CONC  the High Priests Iuwelot and Smendes III in Years 5, 8 and 14, of an anonymous king who can only be Takelot I since Takelot I was their brother. Uniquely, however, the Quay Texts specifically omit any mention of the identity of t
1 CONC he king himself. This suggests that there was a dispute in the royal succession following Osorkon I's death in Upper Egypt, which seriously impaired Takelot I's control there. Harsiese A, as the son of the High Priest Shoshenq C a
1 CONC nd grandson of Osorkon I, or a hypothethical king named Maatkheperre Shoshenq must have appeared as a rival. The Theban priests henceforth, chose to avoid any involvement in this dispute by deliberately leaving the name of the kin
1 CONC g in the Quay Texts 'Blank' rather than choosing sides, as G. Broekman notes in his study of the Karnak Quay Texts. (JEA 88 (2002), pp.170 & 173) This situation was ultimately resolved by Osorkon II who is clearly attested as Phar
1 CONC aoh at Thebes by his 12th Regnal Year, according to Nile Quay Text No.8 and Text No.9.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Gerard Broekman, "The Nile Level Records of the Twenty-Second and Twenty-Third Dynasties in Karnak," JEA 88(2002), pp.163-178.
1 CONT     * Karl Jansen-Winkeln, "Thronname und Begräbnis Takeloth I," Varia Aegyptica 3, (December 1987), pp.253-258.
1 CONT     * Preface to Kenneth Kitchen's 3rd (1996) edition of 'The Third Intermediate Period in Egypt' [pp. xxii-xxiii] which contains an English transcript of Jansen-Winkeln's VA 3(1987) study of Takelot I's tomb.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Shoshenq II Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT 885 – 872 BC
1 CONT Twenty-second dynasty of Egypt Succeeded by:
1 CONT Osorkon II
0 @N6000000002187705529@ NOTE Ashur-dan I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashur-dan I was one of the longest-reigning Kings of Assyria, reigning for some 46 years according to the Assyrian King List (although one version of the list gives him only 36 years). According to one of the more commonly used ch
1 CONC ronologies of the middle Assyrian period, he reigned from 1179 BC to 1133 BC. He succeeded his father, Ninurta-apal-Ekur, and was succeeded by his son, Ninurta-tukulti-Ashur. Beyond this, little is known of his long reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ninurta-apal-Ekur King of Assyria
1 CONT 1179–1133 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ninurta-tukulti-Ashur 
0 @N6000000002187604518@ NOTE Osorkon II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Osorkon II was a pharaoh of the Twenty-second Dynasty Ancient Egypt between 872 BC to c.837 BC who ruled the country from Tanis, the capital of this Dynasty. Osorkon succeeded his father Takelot I to the throne of Egypt but was fa
1 CONC ced with the competing rule of his cousin, king Harsiese A, who controlled both Thebes and the Western Oasis of Egypt. Osorkon feared the serious challenge posed by Harsiese's kingship to his authority, but, when Harsiese convenie
1 CONC ntly died in 860 BC, Osorkon II ensured that this problem would not recur by appointing his own son Nimlot C as High Priest of Amun at Thebes. His younger son Shoshenq was made High Priest of Ptah at Memphis. In this period in Egy
1 CONC pt's history, priestly and political power were at their most inseparable.
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite his astuteness in dealings with matters at home, Osorkon was forced to be more aggressive on the international scene. The growing power of Assyria meant the latter's continued meddling in the affairs of Israel and Syria – 
1 CONC territories well within Egypt's sphere of influence. In 853 BC Osorkon's forces, in a coalition with those of Israel and Byblos, defeated the army of Shalmaneser III at the Battle of Qarqar thereby halting Assyrian expansion in Ca
1 CONC naan, albeit only briefly.
1 CONT
1 CONT Osorkon II devoted considerable resources into building projects – adding to the temple of Bastet at Bubastis which featured a large new hall decorated with scenes depicting his Sed festival and images of his Queen Karomama. Mutem
1 CONC hat was another of his wives. Monumental construction was also performed at Thebes, Memphis, Tanis and Leontopolis. Osorkon II built Temple J at Karnak during the final years of his reign, which was decorated by the then serving H
1 CONC igh Priest Takelot F(the future Takelot II), son of the High Priest Nimlot C. Osorkon II was one of the last great Twenty-second Dynasty kings of Tanis who ruled Egypt from the Delta to Upper Egypt as his successor, Shoshenq III l
1 CONC ost control of Middle and Upper Egypt in his 8th Year with the emergence of Pedubast I there. According to a JEA 81(1995) paper by Karl Jansen-Winkeln, Osorkon was served by the High Priest Harsiese B in his final 3 Years. Jansen-
1 CONC Winkeln also shows that both Harsiese A, and his son [..du] were only ordinary Priest of Amun, rather than High Priests of Amun, as was previously assumed; the inscription on the Koptos lid for [..du], Harsiese A's son, never once
1 CONC  gives him the title of High Priest. The statue CGC 42225, which mentions the High Priest Harsiese B and is dated under Osorkon II, was dedicated by the Letter Writer to Pharaoh Hor IX, who was one of the most powerful men in his 
1 CONC time(see D. Aston JEA 75(1989), p.152). However, Hor IX almost certainly lived during the final years of Osorkon II's reign since he features on Temple J in Karnak which was built late in this Pharaoh's reign, along with the servi
1 CONC ng High Priest Takelot F(son of the High Priest Nimlot C and future Takelot II). Hor IX later served under both Pedubast I and Shoshenq VI.(see p.152 of Aston's paper) This means that the High Priest Harsiese mentioned on statue C
1 CONC GC 42225 must be the second Harsiese – Harsiese B.
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign Length:
1 CONT
1 CONT Osorkon II died around 837 BC and is buried in Tomb NRT I at Tanis. He is now believed to have enjoyed a reign well in excess of 30 Years, rather than just 25 Years. The celebrations of his first Sed Jubilee were traditionally tho
1 CONC ught to have occurred in his 22nd Year but the Heb Sed date in his Great Temple of Bubastis is damaged and can be also be read as Year 30. Recently, it has been demonstrated that Quay Level Text No.14(dated to Year 29 of an Usimar
1 CONC e Setepenamun) belongs to Osorkon II on palaeographical grounds(see page 174-178 of G. Broekman's JEA 88(2002) paper on the Karnak Quay Texts). This finding suggests that Osorkon II likely did celebrate his first Heb Sed in his 30
1 CONC th Year as was traditionally the case with other Libyan era Pharaohs such as Shoshenq III and Shoshenq V. In addition, a Year 22 stela from his reign preserves no mention of any Heb Sed celebrations in this year as one would certa
1 CONC inly expect(see Von Beckerath) While Osorkon II's precise reign length is unknown, some Egyptologists such as Von Beckerath – in his 1997 book Chronology of the Egyptian Pharaohs – and David Aston have suggested a range of 38 to 4
1 CONC 0+ years but these figures cannot be verified.
1 CONT
1 CONT Osorkon II was succeeded by Shoshenq III at Tanis rather than Takelot II Si-Ese as was previously assumed because none of Takelot II's monuments have been found at Lower Egypt where other genuine Tanite kings such as Osorkon II, S
1 CONC hoshenq III and even the short-lived Pami(at 6-7 Years) are mentioned on donation stelas, temple walls and/or annal documents. The only documents which mention a king Takelot--such as a Royal Tomb at Tanis, a Year 9 donation stela
1 CONC  or a heart scarab featuring the nomen Takelot Meryamun--belong exclusively to Takelot I. Aidan Dodson, in his 1994 book, 'The Canopic Equipment of the Kings of Egypt,' observes that Shoshenq III built "a dividing wall, with a dou
1 CONC ble scene showing Osorkon II" and himself "each adoring an unnamed deity" in the antechamber of Osorkon II's tomb.(p.95) Dodson astutely notes that while one may argue that Shoshenq III erected the wall to hide Osorkon II's sarcop
1 CONC hagus, it made no sense for him to create such an elaborate relief if Takelot II had really intervened between him and Osorkon II at Tanis for 25 years unless Shoshenq III was Osorkon II's immediate successor and wished to associa
1 CONC te himself with the deceased king.(p.95) Hence, the case for establishing Takelot II as a Twenty-second Dynasty king and successor to Osorkon II is unproven.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Bibliography:
1 CONT Aidan Dodson, "The Canopic Equipment of the Kings of Egypt," (Kegan Paul Intl: 1994), p.95 
1 CONT David Aston, "Takeloth II: A King of the Theban 23rd Dynasty?," JEA 75(1989), pp.139-53 
1 CONT Gerard Broekman, "The Nile Level Records of the Twenty-Second and Twenty-Third Dynasties in Karnak," JEA 88(2002), pp.170-173 
1 CONT Karl Jansen-Winkeln, "Historische Probleme Der 3. Zwischenzeit," JEA 81(1995) pp.129-149 
0 @N6000000002187705511@ NOTE Mutakkil-Nusku
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mutakkil-Nusku was King of Assyria briefly in 1133 BC. The son of Ashur-dan I, Mutakkil-Nusku usurped the throne from his brother, Ninurta-tukulti-Ashur, apparently only shortly after their father's death. Mutakkil-Nusku died soon
1 CONC  after this act of usurpation, leaving the throne to his son, Ashur-resh-ishi I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ninurta-tukulti-Ashur King of Assyria
1 CONT 1133 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ashur-resh-ishi I 
0 @N6000000002187604463@ NOTE Shoshenq III
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT King Usimare Setepenamun Shoshenq III ruled Egypt's 22nd Dynasty for 39 Years according to contemporary historical records. Two Apis Bulls were buried in Year 4 and Year 28 of his reign and he celebrated his Heb Sed Jubilee in his
1 CONC  Regnal Year 30. Little is known of the precise basis for his successful claim to the throne since he was not a son of Osorkon II and Shoshenq's parentage and family ties are unknown.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Shoshenq III's Regnal Year 8, his reign was marked by the loss of Egypt's political unity, with the appearance of Pedubast I at Thebes. Henceforth, the kings of the 22nd Dynasty only controlled Lower Egypt. The Theban High Pr
1 CONC iest Osorkon B(the future Osorkon III) did date his activities at Thebes and (Upper Egypt) to Shoshenq III's reign but this was solely for administrative reasons since Osorkon did not declare himself king after the death of his fa
1 CONC ther, Takelot II. On the basis of Osorkon B's well known Chronicle document, most Egyptologists today accept that Year 25 of Takelot II is equivalent to Year 22 of Shoshenq III. (see D. Aston, JEA 75(1989), pp.139-153)
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Family
1 CONT
1 CONT Shoshenq III had at least 4 sons–Bakennefi A, Pashedbast B, Pimay who was known as the 'Great Chief of the Ma, son of the Lord of the Two Lands, Shoshenq', and Takeloth C–but they all appear to have predeceased their father throug
1 CONC h his nearly 4 decades-long-rule. Shoshenq's third son, Pimay ('The Lion' in Egyptian), was once thought to be identical with king Pami ('The Cat'), but it is now believed that they are two different individuals, due to the separa
1 CONC te orthography and meaning of their names.
1 CONT
1 CONT Shoshenq III was buried in the looted Royal Tomb NRT V at Tanis.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Osorkon II Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT 837 – 798 BC
1 CONT Twenty-second dynasty of Egypt Succeeded by:
1 CONT Shoshenq IV
0 @N6000000002187988946@ NOTE Shoshenq IV
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hedjkheperre Setepenre Shoshenq IV ruled Egypt's 22nd Dynasty between the reigns of Shoshenq III and Pami. This Pharaoh's existence was first argued by David Rohl but Aidan Dodson settled the issue in a seminal GM 137(1993) articl
1 CONC e. Dodson's GM 137 arguments for the existence of Shoshenq IV are now universally accepted by all Egyptologists, including J. Von Beckerath and Kenneth Kitchen–the latter in the preface to his 1996 3rd edition of the 'Third Interm
1 CONC ediate Period in Egypt.' (page xxvi) While Shoshenq IV shared the same prenomen as his illustrious ancestor Shoshenq I, he is distinguished from Shoshenq I by his use of an especially long nomen–Shoshenq Meryamun Si-Bast Netjerheq
1 CONC awaset/Netjerheqaon which featured both the Si-Bast and Netjerheqawaset/Netjerheqaon epithets. These two epithets were gradually employed by Dynasty 22 Pharaohs starting from the reign of Osorkon II. In contrast, Shoshenq I's nome
1 CONC n reads simply as "Shoshenq Meryamun" while neither Shoshenq I, Osorkon I nor Takelot I ever used any epithets beyond the standard 'Meryamun' (Beloved of Amun) form during their reigns. The British Egyptologist Aidan Dodson--in hi
1 CONC s 1994 book on the Canopic Equipment of the kings of Egypt--perceptively observes that when the Si-Bast epithet "appears during the dynasty of Osorkon II," it is rather infrequent while the Netjerheqawaset/Netjerheqaon epithet is 
1 CONC only exclusively attested "in the reigns of that monarch's successors"(p.93)--ie: Shoshenq III, Shoshenq IV, Pami and Shoshenq V. This means that Shoshenq IV was a late Tanite era king who ruled Egypt after Shoshenq III. His preno
1 CONC men is explicitly attested on a published Year 10 stela(St. Petersburg Hermitage 5630) which bears his unique long form titulary. This stela mentions the Chief of the Libu, Niumateped, who is also attested in office in Year 8 of S
1 CONC hoshenq V. Since the title 'Chief of the Libu' is only first documented in use from Year 31 of Shoshenq III onwards (Dodson, GM 137, p.53), this new king again can only have ruled after Shoshenq III.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Burial
1 CONT
1 CONT Like Shoshenq III, Shoshenq IV was not a son of his predecessor and the exact grounds for his claim to the throne are unknown. He ruled Egypt between Shoshenq III and Pami for either 12 or 13 Years based on different Egyptologists
1 CONC ' interpretation of his reign. According to Dodson, excavation work in the looted NRT V Tanite tomb of Shoshenq III revealed the presence of 2 sarcophagi: one inscribed for Usermaatre Setepenre Shoshenq III and the other being an 
1 CONC anonymous sarcophagus. The unmarked sarcophagus, however, "was clearly a secondary introduction" according to its position in the tomb.(Dodson, GM, p.54) In the tomb's debris, several fragments were found from one or two canopic j
1 CONC ars bearing the name "Hedjkheperre-Setpenre-meryamun-sibast-Netjerheqaon."(Dodson, GM, p.54) Since the epithet Netjerheqaon was never employed by the 22nd Dynasty kings until the reign of Shoshenq III, this constitutes clear evide
1 CONC nce that Shoshenq IV was buried in Shoshenq III's Tanite tomb and succeeded this king. It also demonstrates that the king buried here was certainly not Shoshenq I.
1 CONT
1 CONT NOTE: The original king Shoshenq IV in pre-1993 books and journal articles has been renamed Shoshenq VI by Egyptologists because he was a Theban king who is only attested by Upper Egyptian documents. He was never a king of the Tan
1 CONC ite 22nd Dynasty of Egypt.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Aidan Dodson, "A new King Shoshenq confirmed?" GM 137(1993), pp.53-58.
1 CONT     * Aidan Dodson, "The Canopic Equipment of the Kings of Egypt," (Kegan Paul Intl: 1994), p.93.
1 CONT     * J. Yoyotte, Melanges Maspero I:4 (Cairo:1961), pp.142-43 [Comment on the Year 10 Shoshenq Si-Bast stela].
1 CONT     * Great Chiefs of the Libu: Niumateped
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Shoshenq III Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT 798 – 785 BC
1 CONT Twenty-second dynasty of Egypt Succeeded by:
1 CONT Pami
0 @N6000000002187705492@ NOTE Ashur-resh-ishi I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashur-resh-ishi I was King of Assyria from 1133 to 1115 BC. He succeeded his father, Mutakkil-Nusku, and was succeeded by his son Tiglath-Pileser I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Mutakkil-Nusku King of Assyria
