0 @N6000000002187600454@ NOTE Zariadres
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Zariadres I was King of Sophene.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 201 BC Antiochus III the Great conquers Greater Armenia and Sophene with his Armenian generals(strategoi) Artaxias and Zariadres. Antiochus appointed Zariadres as satrap of Sophene. Following the defeat of Antiochus by the Roma
1 CONC ns at the Battle of Magnesia in 190 BC, Artaxias and Zariadres revolted and, with Roman consent, began to reign autonomously; Artaxias over Greater Armenia and Zariadres over Sophene.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Iranian mythology, Zariadres was the younger brother of Hystaspes, and the hero of the celebrated lovestory of Zariadres and Odatis.
0 @N6000000002188198608@ NOTE Demetrius I of Bactria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Demetrius I was a Greco-Bactrian king (reigned circa 200-180 BCE). He was the son of Euthydemus and succeeded him around 200 BCE, after which he conquered extensive areas in what now is eastern Iran,Iran, Pakistan, Punjab and nort
1 CONC hern India,[1] the thus creating an Indo-Greek kingdom far from Hellenistic Greece. He was posthumously qualified as the Invincible (Aniketos) on the pedigree coins of his successor Agathocles [2] .
1 CONT
1 CONT "Demetrius" was the name of at least two, probably three Greek kings of Bactria and India. The much debated Demetrius II was a possible relative, whereas Demetrios III (c.100 BCE), is known only from numismatic evidence.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Encounter with Antiochus III
1 CONT     * 2 Invasion of India
1 CONT     * 3 Aftermaths
1 CONT     * 4 Demetrius and Buddhism
1 CONT           o 4.1 Coinage & connection with Buddhism
1 CONT           o 4.2 Greco-Buddhist art
1 CONT     * 5 Notes
1 CONT     * 6 External links
1 CONT     * 7 See also
1 CONT     * 8 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Encounter with Antiochus III
1 CONT
1 CONT The father of Demetrius, Euthydemus, was attacked by the Seleucid ruler Antiochus III around 210 BCE. Although he commanded 10,000 horsemen, Euthydemus initially lost a battle on the Arius [3] and had to retreat. He then successfu
1 CONC lly resisted a two-year siege in the fortified city of Bactra, before Antiochus finally decided to recognize the new ruler.
1 CONT
1 CONT The final negociations were made between Antiochus III and Demetrius. Antiochus III was reportedly highly impressed by the demeanour of the young prince, and offered him one of his daughters in marriage, around 206 BCE:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "And after several journeys of Teleas to and fro between the two, Euthydemus at last sent his son Demetrius to confirm the terms of the treaty. Antiochus received the young prince; and judging from his appearance, conversation
1 CONC , and the dignity of his manners that he was worthy of royal power, he first promised to give him one of his own daughters, and secondly conceded the royal title to his father." [4]
1 CONT
1 CONT The term used for "young prince" is neaniskos (?ea??s???), suggesting an age around 16, which in turn gives a birth date for Demetrius around 222 BCE.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Invasion of India
1 CONT Silver obol of Demetrius. Extremely small (12 millimeters in diameter), but beautifully crafted.
1 CONT Silver obol of Demetrius. Extremely small (12 millimeters in diameter), but beautifully crafted.
1 CONT
1 CONT Demetrius started the invasion of northern India from 190 BCE, following the destruction of the Mauryan dynasty by the general Pusyamitra Sunga, who then founded the new Indian Sunga dynasty (185-78 BCE). The Mauryans had had dipl
1 CONC omatic alliances with the Greeks, and they may have been considered as allies by the Greco-Bactrians.
1 CONT
1 CONT Demetrius may have first started to recover the province of Arachosia, an area south of the Hindu Kush already inhabited by many Greek but ruled by the Mauryas since the acquisition of the territory by Chandragupta from Seleucus. 
1 CONC In his "Parthian stations", Isidorus of Charax mentions a colony named Demetrias, supposedly founded by Demetrius himself:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "Beyond is Arachosia. And the Parthians call this White India; there are the city of Biyt and the city of Pharsana and the city of Chorochoad and the city of Demetrias; then Alexandropolis, the metropolis of Arachosia; it is G
1 CONC reek, and by it flows the river Arachotus. As far as this place the land is under the rule of the Parthians." "Parthians stations", 1st century BCE [5]
1 CONT
1 CONT Since Demetrius used the symbol of the elephant scalp from the beginning of his coinage, it is generally thought that he started his victorious campaigns on the Indian territory as a prince, during the rule the rule of his father 
1 CONC Euthydemus. A Greek dedication inscribed on stone and discovered in Kuliab, a hundred kilometers northeast of Ai-Khanoum, also mentionned the victories of the prince Demetrius during the reign of his father:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "Heliodotos dedicated this fragrant altar (...) so that the greatest of all kings Euthydemus, as well as his son, the glorious, victorious and remarkable Demetrius, be preserved of all pains, with the help of the Fortune with 
1 CONC divine thoughts" [6]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Greek campaigns may have gone as far as the capital Pataliputra in eastern India (today Patna): "Those who came after Alexander went to the Ganges and Pataliputra" (Strabo, XV.698)
1 CONT
1 CONT Strabo quotes Demetrius together with Menander, as the ones who went farther into India:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "The Greeks who caused Bactria to revolt grew so powerful on account of the fertility of the country that they became masters, not only of Ariana, but also of India, as Apollodorus of Artemita says: and more tribes were subdue
1 CONC d by them than by Alexander — by Menander in particular (at least if he actually crossed the Hypanis towards the east and advanced as far as the Imaüs), for some were subdued by him personally and others by Demetrius, the son of E
1 CONC uthydemus the king of the Bactrians." (Strabo 11.11.1 [7])
1 CONT
1 CONT It is generally considered that Demetrius ruled in Taxila (where many of his coins were found in the archaeological site of Sirkap), and founded the city of Sagala (Sialkot) in the Punjab (which Ptolemy says is also called "Euthyd
1 CONC emia", probably named by Demetrius in honour of his father).[8]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Indian records also describes Greek attacks on Saketa, Panchala, Mathura and Pataliputra (Gargi-Samhita, Yuga Purana chapter). However, the campaigns to Pataliputra are generally attested to the later king Menander I and Demet
1 CONC rius I probably only invaded areas in Punjab, Kashmir and Pakistan, the latter including areas taken from the Seleucid kings, who were weakened after their defeat to the Romans in 190. Other kings may have expanded the territory a
1 CONC s well.
1 CONT
1 CONT By ca 175 BCE, the Indo-Greeks ruled various parts of northern and northwestern India until the end of the 1st century BCE, while the Sungas remained in the Gangetic, Central, and Eastern India.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Hathigumpha inscription, written by the king of Kalinga, Kharavela, has been interpreted to describe the presence of the Greek king "Demetrius" with his army in eastern India, possibly as far as the city of Rajagriha about 70 
1 CONC km southeast of Pataliputra and one of the foremost Buddhist sacred cities, but claims that Demetrius ultimately retreated to Mathura on hearing of Kharavela's military successes further south:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "Then in the eighth year, (Kharavela) with a large army having sacked Goradhagiri causes pressure on Rajagaha (Rajagriha). On account of the loud report of this act of valour, the Yavana (Greek) King Dimi[ta] retreated to Math
1 CONC ura having extricated his demoralized army." Epigraphia Indica, Vol. XX [9].
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Aftermaths
1 CONT
1 CONT Demetrius I died of unknown reasons, and the date 180 BCE, is merely a suggestion aimed to allow suitable regnal periods for subsequent kings, of which there were several. Even if some of them were co-regents, civil wars and tempo
1 CONC rary divisions of the empire are most likely.
1 CONT
1 CONT The kings Pantaleon, Antimachus, Agathocles and possibly Euthydemus II ruled after Demetrius I, and theories about their origin include all of them being relatives of Demetrius I, or only Antimachus. Eventually, the kingdom of Bac
1 CONC tria fell to the able newcomer Eucratides.
1 CONT
1 CONT Demetrius II was a later king, possibly a son or nephew of his namesake, and he ruled in India only. Justin mentions him being defeated by the Bactrian king Eucratides, an event which took place at the end of the latters reign, po
1 CONC ssibly around 150 BCE. Demetrius II left behind his generals Apollodotus and Menander I, who in turn became kings of India and rulers of the Indo-Greek Kingdom following his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Ptolemy, a Demetriapolis was founded in Arachosia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Demetrius is a legend as well as an enigma. He was mentioned by Geoffrey Chaucer ("D, lord of Ind").
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Demetrius and Buddhism
1 CONT The other main coin type of Demetrius. Obv: Rejoincing young elephant with bell, within the royal bead and reel contour. Rev: Caduceus, representing concord between two serpents. Greek legend ??S???OS ????????? (BASILEOS DEMETRIOU
1 CONC ) "Of King Demetrius".
1 CONT The other main coin type of Demetrius.
1 CONT Obv: Rejoincing young elephant with bell, within the royal bead and reel contour.
1 CONT Rev: Caduceus, representing concord between two serpents. Greek legend ??S???OS ????????? (BASILEOS DEMETRIOU) "Of King Demetrius".
1 CONT Greco-Buddhist representation of Buddha, Gandhara, 1st-2nd century CE.
1 CONT Greco-Buddhist representation of Buddha, Gandhara, 1st-2nd century CE.
1 CONT
1 CONT There are many records of the Sunga empire persecuting Buddhism, but on the contrary Buddhism flourished under the Indo-Greek kings, and it has been suggested by W.W. Tarn that their invasion of India was not only intended to show
1 CONC  their support for the Mauryan empire, but also to protect the Buddhist faith from the religious persecutions of the Sungas.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Coinage & connection with Buddhism
1 CONT
1 CONT The coins of Demetrius are of four types. One bilingual type with Greek and Kharoshthi legends exists: it is naturally associated with the Indian Demetrius II. A series with the king in diadem are likely to be early issues of Deme
1 CONC trius I.
1 CONT
1 CONT More interesting are the "elephant" coins: The first type shows Demetrius (I) with elephant-crown, a well-known symbol of India and an allusion to Alexander the Great. These coins prove he really invaded India.
1 CONT
1 CONT The other "elephant" type of Demetrius I represents a rejoicing elephant, depicted on the front on the coin and surrounded by the royal bead-and-reel decoration, and therefore treated on the same level as a King. The elephant, one
1 CONC  of the symbols of Buddhism and the Gautama Buddha, possibly represents the victory of Buddhism brought about by Demetrius. The reverse of the coin depicts the caduceus, symbol of reconciliation between two fighting serpents, whic
1 CONC h is likely a representation of peace between the Greeks and the Sungas, and likewise between Buddhism and Hinduism. It might also be a symbol of Asklepios, the Greek deity of medicine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alternatively though, the elephant has also been described as a possible symbol of the Greek Indian capital of Taxila (Tarn), or still as a symbol of India. Unambiguous Buddhist symbols are found on later Greek coins, but it seems
1 CONC  rather unlikely that Demetrius I, who was born in the Greek milieu of Bactria and struck coins with Olympic gods, personally was a buddhist. His conquests did however influence the Buddhist religion in India:
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Greco-Buddhist art
1 CONT
1 CONT There are several parallels between Demetrius and the first representations of the Buddha in human form.
1 CONT
1 CONT The deified likeness of Demetrius, including facial features and physical proportions, may have influenced some of the first representations of the Buddha in Greco-Buddhist art, typically depicted in a manner reminiscent of the Gr
1 CONC eek king.
1 CONT
1 CONT Also in another parallel, the characteristic protector deity of Demetrius (Herakles standing with his club over his arm, as seen on the reverse of his coins), was represented in the Greco-Buddhist art of Gandhara as the protector 
1 CONC deity of the Buddha.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Euthydemus I Greco-Bactrian and
1 CONT Indo-Greek ruler
1 CONT (205-171 BCE) Succeeded by:
1 CONT (possible sub-kings)
1 CONT
1 CONT (in Bactria)
1 CONT Euthydemus II
1 CONT
1 CONT (in Paropamisadae)
1 CONT Agathocles
1 CONT
1 CONT (in Arachosia, Gandhara)
1 CONT Pantaleon
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Demetrius is said to have founded Taxila (archaeological excavations), and also Sagala in the Punjab, which he seemed to have called Euthydemia, after his father ("the city of Sagala, also called Euthydemia" (Ptolemy, Geog
1 CONC raphia, VII 1))
1 CONT    2. ^ No undisputed coins of Demetrius I himself use this title, but it is employed on one of the pedigree coins issued by Agathocles, which bear on the reverse the classical profile of Demetrius crowned by the elephant scalp, w
1 CONC ith the legend DEMETRIOU ANIKITOU, and on the reverse Herakles crowning himself, with the legend "Of king Agathocles" (Boppearachchi, Pl 8). Coins of the supposed Demetrius III also use the title "Invincible", and therefore are at
1 CONC tributed by some to the same Demetrius (Whitehead and al.)
1 CONT    3. ^ Polybius 10.49, Battle of the Arius
1 CONT    4. ^ Polybius 11.34 Siege of Bactra
1 CONT    5. ^ Mentioned in Bopearachchi, "Monnaies Greco-Bactriennes et Indo-Grecques", p52. Original text in paragraph 19 of Parthian stations
1 CONT    6. ^ Heliodotos inscription, in "Afghanistan, ancien carrefour entre l'Est et Ouest", p133. ISBN 2503516815
1 CONT    7. ^ Strabo 11.11.1 full text
1 CONT    8. ^ Francine Tissot, "Gandhara", 2002, ISBN 2720010316
1 CONT    9. ^ Full text of the Hathigumpta inscription
1 CONT
