0 @N6000000002188193546@ NOTE Aminah bint Wahhab
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Aminah bint Wahab (Arabic: ???? ??? ???? translit: 'Aminah bint Wahab) was the mother of Muhammad, the prophet of Islam.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography
1 CONT
1 CONT She was a member of the Banu Zuhrah clan.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT She was the daughter of Wahab ibn Abd al-Manaf, and was married to Abdallah ibn Abd al-Muttalib. There are also texts which claim that Aminah bint Wahab died in a trip to Yathrib while she took Muhammad to meet his uncles and to s
1 CONC ee the grave of his father. Aminah died in 577.
0 @N6000000002188193557@ NOTE Muhammad
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad (Arabic: ????? mu?ammad; also Mohammed, Mohamet, and other variants[1][2]) (570-632 AD)[3][4] was an Arab religious, political and military leader who founded Islam and the Muslim community (Arabic: ??? Ummah). He united 
1 CONC the tribes of the Arabian Peninsula into a federation of allied tribes with its capital at Medina.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Islamic traditions, Muhammad began receiving revelations from God (Arabic: ???? Allah) from the age of 40, delivered through the angel Gabriel over the last 23 years of his life. The content of these revelations, know
1 CONC n as the Qur'an,[5] was memorized and recorded by his followers and compiled into a single Book shortly after his death. The Qur'an, along with the details of Muhammad’s life as recounted by his biographers and his contemporaries,
1 CONC  forms the basis of Islamic theology. Within Islam, he is considered the last and most important prophet of God.[6] Muslims do not regard him as the founder of a new religion but as the restorer of the original monotheistic faith 
1 CONC of Adam, Abraham and other prophets, [7][8][9][10][11] whose messages, according to some, had become misinterpreted or corrupted over time. [12]
1 CONT
1 CONT After Muhammad, a rapid creation of an empire under the Umayyads established a new polity from the Atlantic to the Indus River. Within a few decades of his death, his successors united all of Arabia in an Islamic empire which conq
1 CONC uered the Sassanid and Byzantine empires. With historically unprecedented swiftness, they conquered present-day Iran, Iraq, Egypt, Palestine, Syria, Armenia, and most of Byzantine-held North Africa. The Caliphate soon pressed on t
1 CONC o the Iberian Peninsula in the west, and much of Central Asia and Sindh (present day Pakistan) in the east. By 750, Islam was as fully established as the other two Abrahamic belief systems, Judaism and Christianity, and at which t
1 CONC ime, it was the world's greatest military power.[citation needed]
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Etymology
1 CONT     * 2 Overview
1 CONT     * 3 Sources for Muhammad's life
1 CONT     * 4 Modern Western Academic view of Muhammad
1 CONT     * 5 Life based on Islamic traditions
1 CONT           o 5.1 Before Medina
1 CONT                 + 5.1.1 Genealogy
1 CONT                 + 5.1.2 Childhood
1 CONT                 + 5.1.3 Middle years
1 CONT                 + 5.1.4 The Beginnings of the Qur'an
1 CONT                 + 5.1.5 Rejection
1 CONT                 + 5.1.6 Isra and Miraj
1 CONT                 + 5.1.7 Hijra to Ethiopia
1 CONT           o 5.2 Muhammad in Medina
1 CONT                 + 5.2.1 Hijra to Medina
1 CONT                 + 5.2.2 Beginnings of conflict
1 CONT                 + 5.2.3 The conflict with Mecca
1 CONT                 + 5.2.4 Muhammad and the Jewish tribes of Medina
1 CONT                 + 5.2.5 The truce of Hudaybiyya
1 CONT                 + 5.2.6 Muhammad's letters to the Heads of State
1 CONT           o 5.3 Conquest of Mecca
1 CONT                 + 5.3.1 Unification of Arabia
1 CONT                 + 5.3.2 Death
1 CONT           o 5.4 Muhammad as a military leader
1 CONT           o 5.5 Family life
1 CONT           o 5.6 Companions
1 CONT     * 6 Muhammad the reformer
1 CONT     * 7 Miracles in the Muslim biographies
1 CONT     * 8 Traditional views of Muhammad
1 CONT           o 8.1 Seal of the Prophets
1 CONT           o 8.2 More traditions
1 CONT           o 8.3 Depictions of Muhammad
1 CONT           o 8.4 Muslim veneration of Muhammad
1 CONT           o 8.5 Other religious traditions in regard to Muhammad
1 CONT     * 9 See also
1 CONT     * 10 Notes
1 CONT     * 11 References
1 CONT           o 11.1 Encyclopedias
1 CONT     * 12 Further reading
1 CONT     * 13 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT Etymology
1 CONT "Muhammad" in Arabic calligraphy.
1 CONT "Muhammad" in Arabic calligraphy.
1 CONT
1 CONT The name Muhammad etymologically means "the praised one" in Arabic.[13] Within Islam, Muhammad is known as Nabi (Prophet) and Rasul (Messenger). Although the Qur'an sometimes declines to make a distinction among prophets, in verse
1 CONC  33:40 it singles out Muhammad as the "Seal of the Prophets" (33:40).[14] The Qur'an also refers to Muhammad as "Ahmad" (61:6) (Arabic :????), Arabic for "more praiseworthy".
1 CONT
1 CONT Overview
1 CONT
1 CONT Born to ‘Abdu’llah ibn ‘Abdu’l-Muttalib, Muhammad initially adopted the occupation of a shepherd, and later became a merchant. In his youth, he was called by the nickname "Al-Amin" (Arabic: ?????? ), a common Arab name meaning "fa
1 CONC ithful, trustworthy" and was sought out as an impartial arbitrator.[9][4] During the month of Ramadan, Muhammad would retreat to a cave located at the summit of Mount Hira, just outside Mecca in the Arabian Hijaz, where he fasted 
1 CONC and prayed. According to Islamic belief, when Muhammad was about forty (610 CE), Archangel Gabriel visited him in the cave and commanded him to recite verses sent by God. These revelations continued until his death twenty-three ye
1 CONC ars later. The collection of these verses is known as the Qur'an.
1 CONT
1 CONT He expanded his mission as a prophet, publicly preaching strict monotheism, preaching against the social evils of his day, and warning of a Day of Judgment when all humans shall be held responsible for their deeds.[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT After ignoring Muhammad's preaching, the elites in Mecca, commercially threatened by the growing popularity of his message, persecuted Muhammad and his followers. This continued, and intensified, over more than a decade. The hards
1 CONC hips reached a new level for Muhammad after the deaths of his wife Khadija and his uncle Abu Talib, an important political protector of Muhammad. Eventually, in 622, he left Mecca in a journey known to Muslims as the Hijra (the Mi
1 CONC gration).[4] He settled in the area of Yathrib (now known as Medina) with his followers, where he was the leader of the first Muslim community.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eight years of war between Muhammad and Meccan forces followed, ending with the Muslim victory and conquest of Mecca. The Muslims subsequently removed everything they considered idolatrous from the Kaaba. Most of the townspeople a
1 CONC ccepted Islam. In March 632, Muhammad led the pilgrimage known as the Hajj. On returning to Medina he fell ill and died after a few days, on June 8.
1 CONT
1 CONT Under the caliphs who assumed authority after his death, the Islamic empire expanded into Palestine, Syria, Mesopotamia, Persia, Egypt, North Africa, southern Spain, and Anatolia. Later conquests, commercial contact between Muslim
1 CONC s and non-Muslims, and missionary activity spread Islam over much of the Eastern Hemisphere, including China and Southeast Asia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources for Muhammad's life
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Historiography of early Islam
1 CONT
1 CONT The dates often given for Muhammad's life are 570-632 AD/CE. The earliest surviving biography of Muhammad is a collection of hadith called the Sirah Rasul Allah or, the Life of the Apostle of God, by Ibn Ishaq, a member of the Tab
1 CONC i‘in generation who was born 85 years after Hijra — approximately 717 — and who died in 767.
1 CONT
1 CONT Other sources for biographies of Muhammad are:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * the Ibn Hisham's Prophetic Sirah (Arabic: ?????? ???????), which is a detailed trace of the Prophet's lineage and life
1 CONT     * the military chronicles of Waqidi (745-822)
1 CONT     * the biographies of Ibn Sa'd (783-845), a student of Waqidi
1 CONT     * later histories
1 CONT     * Qur'anic commentaries
1 CONT     * collections of Prophetic hadith
1 CONT
1 CONT These texts were recorded more than a century, and often several centuries, after the death of Muhammad. The Qur'an is generally considered by academic scholars to record the words spoken by Muhammad.[15] The Qur'an (a word that l
1 CONC iterally translates as "Recitation") was also maintained by the "Hafiz", people who memorized the entire document and recited it.
1 CONT
1 CONT Modern Western Academic view of Muhammad
1 CONT The neutrality of this section is disputed.
1 CONT Please see the discussion on the talk page.
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Non-Islamic view of Muhammad's historicity
1 CONT
1 CONT 11th century Persian Qur'an folio page in kufic script
1 CONT 11th century Persian Qur'an folio page in kufic script
1 CONT
1 CONT From a scholarly point of view, the most credible source providing information on events in Muhammad's life is the Qur'an, even though its chronological interpretation is complex, and even though it contains very little in the way
1 CONC  of coherent biography.[citation needed] Next in importance are the traditional Muslim biographies of Muhammad and quotes attributed to him (the sira and hadith literature), which provide further information on Muhammad's life.[16
1 CONC ] All, or most, of the Qur'an was apparently written down by Muhammad's secretaries while he was alive, but it was, then as now, primarily an orally related document, and the written compilation of the whole Qur'an in its definite
1 CONC  form as we have it now was completed early after the death of Muhammad.[17] The earliest surviving written sira (Biographies of Muhammad and quotes attributed to him) dates to 150 years after Muhammad, the compilation and (critic
1 CONC al) analysis of which took place even later.[18]
1 CONT
1 CONT Modern historians agree that Muhammad lived during the 7th century and adopted various monotheistic traditions in an effort to replace the common polytheistic religions of the Arabian Peninsula, eventually gaining wide acceptance 
1 CONC as a prophet.[citation needed]
1 CONT
