0 @N6000000004533305239@ NOTE Musa ibn Nusair, Abú Abd al-Rahmán
1 CONT
1 CONT (el moro Muza ) Gobernador musulmán del norte de África que dirigió laocupación de la península Ibérica (Yemen, h. 640 - Damasco ?,716/718). Era un liberto del walí de Egipto, hermano del sultán Omeya,que le nombró gobernador de l
1 CONC as provincias occidentales de Ifriqiya yel Magreb (704). Completó la islamización de la segunda y la extendiócon la conquista de Marruecos. Fue entonces cuando el conde donJulián, gobernador visigodo de Ceuta y miembro del partido
1 CONC  vitizano,contrario al rey Rodrigo, convenció a Musa para que pasara con sustropas a España y ayudara a derrocarle. Musa envió a Tarif ben Malluken una primera expedición de reconocimiento, rechazada por las fuerzasde Rodrigo (710
1 CONC ), y a su lugarteniente, Tariq ben Ziyad, en unasegunda definitiva, ya autorizada por el califa (711). La rápidapenetración de Tariq en el territorio peninsular aconsejó a Musa pasarpersonalmente a al-Ándalus para consolidar la co
1 CONC nquista en el 712. Conayuda de los vitizanos fue tomando ciudades sobrepasadas en su avancepor Tariq, dirigiendo su ejército desde Algeciras hasta Sevilla,Mérida, Toledo, Zaragoza, Asturias y Lugo. Pero en el 714 hubo deregresar a
1 CONC  Damasco ante los insistentes requerimientos del califaSulaymán, dejando como gobernador de al-Ándalus a su hijo Abd al-Aziz.Al llegar a Damasco halló a un nuevo califa, Sulaymán, que le condenópor la forma en que había repartido 
1 CONC el botín de sus conquistas. Nopudo regresar nunca a al-Ándalus y murió oscuramente.
1 CONT © Biografías y Vidas, 2004.
0 @N6000000002188182814@ NOTE Marwan I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Marwan ibn al-Hakam (623 - 685) (Arabic: ????? ?? ?????) was the fourth Ummayad Caliph, who took over the dynasty after Muawiya II gave up the title in 684. Marwan's ascension pointed to a shift in the lineage of the Umayyad dynas
1 CONC ty from descendants of Abu Sufyan to those of Hakam, both of whom were grandsons of Umayya (for whom the Umayyad dynasty is named). Hakam was a first cousin of Uthman ibn Affan.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Prophet Muhammad sent both him and his father, Hakam ibn al-Aas to exile, saying they should never return. During the reign of Uthman, he sent Marwan and possibly his father back. Marwan took advantage of his relationship to t
1 CONC he caliph and was appointed governor of Medina. A number of early historians such as Ibn Taymiyya and the Sunnis in general question the authenticity of this story, which doesn't come from any Sahih hadiths or from a reputable wri
1 CONC ter from soon after Muhammad's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the "Battle of the Camel" Marwan ibn al-Hakam is said to have shot his general Talha with an arrow to the thigh, resuting in his death. Marwan killed Talha in revenge for Talha's alleged betrayal of the third Caliph Uthman:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "I saw this man (Talha) during the days when Uthman was besieged in his house. He was inciting and urging the crowd to enter the house, and to kill him." 23
1 CONT
1 CONT He was removed from this position by Ali, only to be reappointed by Muawiya I. Marwan was eventually removed from the city when Abdullah ibn Zubayr rebelled against Yazid I. From here, Marwan went to Damascus, where he was made th
1 CONC e caliph after Muawiya II abdicated.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marwan's short reign was marked by a civil war among the Umayyads as well as a war against Abdullah bin Zubayr who continued to rule over the Hejaz, Iraq, Egypt and parts of Syria. Marwan was able to win the Umayyad civil war, the
1 CONC  result of which was a new Marwanid line of Umayyad caliphs. He was also able to recapture Egypt and Syria from Abdullah, but was not able to completely defeat him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Note that Shi'a do not consider any of the Umayyad caliphs or any of the three predecessors to Ali to be legitimate. See Succession to Muhammad for more details.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Muawiya II Caliph
1 CONT 684–685 Succeeded by
1 CONT Abd al-Malik
